\id 2MA Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Machabees
\toc1 The Second Book of Machabees.
\toc2 2 Machabees
\toc3 2MA
\mt1 The Second Book of Machabees.
\c 1
\cd The Iewes in Ieruſalem write to the Iewes in Ægypt, 7. ſignifying that they had writte before of manie afflictions paſt: 9. and now write againe, rendering thankes to God for their deliuerie from Antiochus: 18. exhorting their bretheren to kepe the feaſt of Scenopegia: reciting the miracle of water returning into fire: 24. when Nehemias prayed: 33. and that the king built a temple in memorie therof.
\p
\v 1 TO \f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft E\f*\f 2 \fr 1.1 \ft This Appendix concerning two epiſtles of the Iewes was added to the hiſtorie, of the firſt booke by him that writte this ſecond.\f* THE bretheren, the Iewes that are through out Ægypt, the bretheren, the Iewes that are in Ieruſalem, and that are in the countrie of Iurie, ſend health, and good peace.
\v 2 God doe good to you, and remember his teſtament, that he ſpake to Abraham, and Iſaac, and Iacob, his faithful ſeruantes:
\v 3 and geue he vnto you al an hart to worshippe him, and to doe his wil with a great hart, and a willing minde.
\v 4 Open he your hart in his law, and in his precepts, and make peace.
\v 5 Heare he your prayers, and be he reconciled vnto you, neither forſake he you in the euil time.
\v 6 And now here we are praying for you.
\v 7 When Demetrius reigned, in the yeare one hundreth ſixtie nine, \f a \fr 1.7 \ft As they had vvritten before in their afflictions, ſo novv they exhorte their bretheren to be thankful to God, and amongſt other meanes to shevv their gratful minde by celebrating the feaſt of dedication of the nevv altar.\f* we Iewes wrote vnto you in the tribulation, and violence, that came vpon vs in theſe yeares, ſince Iaſon reuolted from the holie land, and from the kingdom. \f 1 \fr 1.7 \ft li. 2. c. 4. v. 7.\f*
\v 8 They burnt the gate, and shed innocent bloud: and we prayed to our Lord, and were heard, and we offered ſacrifice, and fine floore, and lighted the lampes, and ſetforth there breades.
\v 9 And now celebrate ye the dayes of Scenopegia of the moneth Ceſleu.
\v 10 In the yeare one hundreth eightie eight, the people that is at Ieruſalem, and in Iurie, and the ſenate, and \f b \fr 1.10 \ft It ſemeth that either this Iudas vvas the firſt ſonne of Iohn Hyrcamus (othervviſe called Ariſtobolus) or rather Iudas Eſſenus of vvhom, ch. 2, v. 14. and of vvhom Ioſephus vvriteth, \+it li. 13. c. 19\+it*.\f* Iudas, to Ariſtobolus the maiſter of Ptolomee the king, who is of the ſtocke of the annointed prieſtes, and to thoſe Iewes, that are in Ægypt health, and welfare.
\v 11 Being deliuered by God out of great dangers, we geue him thankes magnifically, as who haue fought againſt ſuch a king.
\v 12 For he made them ſwarme out of Perſis, that haue fought againſt vs, and the holie citie.
\v 13 For when he was captaine in Perſis, & with him a very great armie, he fel in the temple of Nania, being deceiued by the counſel of Naneaes prieſtes.
\v 14 For Antiochus, & his freindes came to the place as to dwel with her, & that he might receiue much money vnder the title of a dowrie.
\v 15 And when Naneaes prieſtes had layde it forth, and he with a few was entered within the compaſſe of the temple, they shut the temple,
\v 16 when Antiochus was entered in: & a ſecrete entrance of the temple being opened, caſting ſtones they ſtroke the duke, and them that were with him, and diuided them in peeces, and cutting of their heades they threw them forth.
\v 17 Bleſſed be the God in al thinges, who hath deliuered vp the impious.
\v 18 We therefore meaning to keepe the purification of the temple the fiue and twentith day of the moneth Caſleu, thought it neceſſary to ſignifie vnto you: that you alſo may kepe the day of Scenopegia, and the day of the fire, that was geuen when Nehemias, after the temple was built and the altar, offered ſacrifice.
\v 19 For when our fathers were caryed \f c \fr 1.19 \ft Chaldea being nere to Perſis, is ſometimes comprehended vnder the ſame name ſo Mamertinus, \+it in Panagerica ad Iulia\+it*. reciteth \+it Tigris and Euphrates\+it* amongſt the riuers of Perſi. & S. Chryſoſtom. \+it ho. 6. in Math\+it*. ſaith the Ievves vvere deliuered \+it from captiuitie of Perſis\+it*, meaning Babylon.\f* into Perſis, the prieſtes that then were the worshippers of God, taking the fire from the altar, hid it ſecretly in a valley where there was a pitte deepe, and dry, and therin they ſaued it, ſo that the place was vnknowne to al men.
\v 20 But when manie yeares had paſſed, and it pleaſed God that Nehemias should be ſent of the king of Perſis: he ſent the nephewes of thoſe prieſtes, that had hid it, to ſeeke out the fire: and as they told vs, they found not fire, but \f d \fr 1.20 \ft In this fire vvere foure miracles. Firſt it vvas not changed into ayre but into vvater. \+it v. 20\+it*.\f* thicke water.
\v 21 And he bad them draw, & bring vnto him: and the ſacrifices, that were layd on, the prieſt Nehemias commanded to be ſprinckled with the ſame water, and the wood, and the thinges that were layde therupon.
\v 22 And as this was done, & the time was come that the ſunne shone forth, which before was in a cloude, there \f e \fr 1.22 \ft Secondly this vvater being caſt on the hoſtes of ſacrifice was kindled as fire. \+it v. 22\+it*.\f* was kindled a great fire, ſo that al merueiled.
\v 23 And al the prieſtes made prayer, whiles the ſacrifice was conſuming, Ionathas beginning, and the reſt anſwering.
\v 24 And the prayer of Nehemias was in this maner: \sc O LORD\sc* God creatour of al, dreadful, and ſtrong, iuſt, and merciful, which only art the good king,
\v 25 only giuer, only iuſt, and omnipotent, and eternal, which deliuereſt Iſrael from al euil, which madeſt the fathers elect, and didſt ſanctifie them.
\v 26 Receiue the ſacrifice for al thy people Iſrael, and kepe thy part, and ſanctifie it.
\v 27 Gather together our diſperſion, deliuer them, that ſerue the Gentils, and regard the contemned & abhorred: that the Gentils may know that thou art our God.
\v 28 Afflict them that oppreſſe vs, and that doe contumelie in pride.
\v 29 Place thy people in thy holie place, as Moyſes ſayd.
\v 30 And the prieſtes ſang hymnes, til the ſacrifice was conſumed.
\v 31 And when the ſacrifice was conſumed, of the reſt of the water Nehemias commanded the greater ſtones to be throughly washed.
\v 32 Which thing as it was done, out of them \f f \fr 1.32 \ft Thirdly, it burned alſo being caſt vpon ſtones. \+it v. 32\+it*.\f* was kindled a flame: but \f g \fr 1.32 \ft Fourth, it was extinguished by the light that came from the altar. \+it v. 32\+it*.\f* by light alſo, that shined from the altar, it was conſumed.
\v 33 And as the thing was made manifeſt, it was told the king of the Perſians, that in the place, wherin thoſe prieſtes that were tranſported, had hid fire, there appeared water, with the which Nehemias, and they that were with him, purified the ſacrifices.
\v 34 And the king conſidering, and diligently examining the matter, made a temple for it, that he might approue that which was done.
\v 35 And when he had approued it, he gaue to the prieſtes manie good thinges, and diuerſe giftes, and taking them with his owne hand, he gaue to them.
\v 36 And Nehemias called this place \f h \fr 1.36 \ft \+it Nephthar\+it*, ſignifieth \+it deliuerie\+it*, which is the effect of purification, for the temple & other holie thinges being purified, were deliuered from prophane vſe.\f* Nephthar, which is interpreted purification. But it is called with manie Nephi.
\c 2
\cd Jeremy the Prophet, in the tranſmigration into Babylon. 4. Likewiſe the Tabernacle of Moyſes, the Arke, and Altar of incenſe in a ſecrete place. 8. As Moyſes had dedicated the Tabernacle, and Salomon the Temple: 14. ſo Iudas Machabeus clenſing the Temple, and making a new Altar, inſtituted a feaſt of the Dedication therof. 20. The Preface of the Auctor abridging the hiſtorie of the Machabees written by Iaſon in fiue bookes.
\p
\v 1 AND it is found in the deſcriptions of Ieremie the prophet, that he commanded them that went in tranſmigration, to take \f a \fr 2.1 \ft S. Ambroſe, \+it li. 3. c. 14. Offic\+it*. vvriteth at large of this miracle.\f* the fire, as it was ſignified, & as he commanded them that were caried away in tranſmigration.
\v 2 And he gaue them a law that they should not forget the precepts of our Lord, and that they should not erre in their mindes, ſeing the idols of gold, and ſiluer, and the ornaments of them.
\v 3 And ſaying other like thinges, he exhorted them that they would not remoue the law from their hart.
\v 4 \f 1 \fr 2.4 \ft \+it Epis. Iere. Baruc. 6.\+it*\f* And it was in the ſame writing, how the prophet commanded by the diuine anſwer made to him, that \f b \fr 2.4 \ft Neither the tabernacle nor the arke vvere caried avvay by Nabuchodonoſor, but vvere preſerued by ſome meanes: and moſt like (beſides the auctoritie of this ſcripture) by Ieremie the prophet, vvho had ſpecial fauour of the Babylonians, vvhen Ieruſalem vvas taken. \+it Iere. 32. v. 11\+it*.\f* the tabernacle, & the arke should folow in company with him, til he came forth into the mount in which Moyſes aſcended, and ſaw the inheritance of God.
\v 5 And Ieremie coming thither found a place of a caue: & he brought the tabernacle, and the arke, and the altar of incenſe in thither, and ſtopped the doore.
\v 6 And there came certaine withal, that folowed, to marke the place for themſelues: and they could not finde it.
\v 7 And as Ieremie vnderſtood it, blaming them he ſayd: that the place shal be vnknowne, til God gather together the congregation of the people, and become propicious:
\v 8 \x - \xt Exo. 40. 3. Reg. 8.\x* and then our Lord wil shew theſe thinges, and the maieſtie of our Lord shal appeare, & there shal be a cloude as it was alſo made manifeſt to Moyſes, and as when Salomon prayed that the place might be ſanctified to the great God, he did manifeſt theſe thinges.
\v 9 For he handled wiſedom magnifically: as hauing wiſedome, did he offer the ſacrifice of the dedication, and of the conſummation of the temple.
\v 10 As Moyſes alſo prayed to our Lord, and as Salomon prayed, and fire came downe from heauen, and conſumed the holocauſt.
\v 11 And Moyſes ſayd: Becauſe that which was for ſinne, was not eaten, it was conſumed. \x - \xt Levit. 9. v. 24.\x*
\v 12 In like maner Salomon alſo eight dayes celebrated the dedication.
\v 13 And theſe ſelf ſame thinges were put in the deſcriptions, and commentaries of Nehemias: and after what ſort he making a librarie gathered together bookes of the prophetes, and of Dauid, and the epiſtles of the kinges, and concerning the donaries.
\v 14 And in like maner \f c \fr 2.14 \ft This ſemeth to be the ſame Iudas Eſſenus vvho vvith others vvritte this epiſtle, \+it ch. 1. v. 10\+it*.\f* Iudas alſo ſuch thinges as were fallen away by the warre that happened to vs, gathered them al together, and they are with vs.
\v 15 If therfore you deſire theſe thinges, ſend ſome that may fetch them vnto you.
\v 16 We therfore meaning to kepe the purification haue written vnto you: You shal doe wel then, if you kepe theſe dayes.
\v 17 And God that hath deliuered his people, and rendered the inheritance to al, the kingdom, and the prieſthoode, and the ſanctification,
\v 18 as he promiſed in the law, we hope that he wil quickly haue mercie vpon vs, and wil gather vs together from vnder heauen into the holie place.
\v 19 For he hath deliuered vs out of great perils, and hath purged the place. \f 1 \fr 2.19 \ft \+it li. 2. c. 15. v. 38\+it*.\f*
\v 20 \f 1 \fr 2.20 \ft After the auctor had vvritten this Appendix to the former booke, he reſolued alſo to adde an abridgement of the vvhole hiſtorie, vvherunto he maketh this Preface, ſignifying the matter vvherof he vvil vvrite. v. 20. in vvhat maner, v. 24. and why, v. 25. auouching his diligence. v. 26. vvith breuitie. v. 29.\f*\f d \fr 2.20 \ft See previous note\f* Concerning Iudas Machabeus, and his brethren, and of the purification of the great temple, and of the dedication of the altar,
\v 21 Yea and of the battels that perteyne to Antiochus the Noble, and his ſonne Eupator:
\v 22 and of the apparitions, that were made from heauen to them, that did manfully for the Iewes, ſo that being few they reuenged the whole countrie, and put to flight the barbarous multitude:
\v 23 and repayred the moſt famous temple in al the world, and deliuered the citie, and the lawes that were abolished, were reſtored, our Lord with al clemencie being made propicious vnto them.
\v 24 Alſo the thinges which by Iaſon the Cyrenean, are compriſed in fiue bookes we haue attempted to abridge in one volume.
\v 25 For conſidering the multitude of bookes, and \f e \fr 2.25 \ft God aſſiſting the vvriters of diuine hiſtories, doth not alvvayes deliuer them from labour in ſeeking to knovv the ſame of ſuch as knew particular thinges. So S. Luke vvritte the Goſpel, \+it hauing diligently attained to al thinges Luc. 1. v. 3\+it*.\f* the difficultie to them, that wil attempt the narrations of hiſtories, becauſe of the multitude of matters:
\v 26 we haue bene careful for them that are willing to reade, that there might be delectation of the minde: and for the ſtudious, that they may more eaſily comitte it to memorie: and that profite might enſewe to al that read it.
\v 27 And to our owne ſelues in deede, which haue taken vpon vs this worke to make an abridgement, we haue taken in hand no eaſie labour, yea rather a buſines ful of watching and ſwette.
\v 28 As they that prepare a feaſt, and ſeeke to condeſcend to other mens wil: for manie mens ſakes we doe willingly ſuſteyne the labour.
\v 29 The \f 1 \fr 2.29 \ft \+it exact declaration\+it*.\f* veritie certes concerning euery particular leauing to the auctors, and our ſelues according to the forme that is geuen, ſtudying to be briefe.
\v 30 For as the builder of a new houſe muſt haue care of the whole building: but he that hath the care to paint, muſt ſeeke out the thinges that are apt for garnishing: ſo muſt it be eſteemed alſo in vs.
\v 31 For to gather together the vnderſtanding, to order the ſpeach, and curiouſly to diſcuſſe euerie particular part, agreeth to the auctor of an hiſtorie:
\v 32 but to purſew the breuitie of ſpeach, & to auoyde the exact declarations of thinges, is to be granted to him that abridgeth.
\v 33 From hence then wil we begine the narration: of the preface let it be ſufficient to haue ſayd thus much. for it is a foolish thing before the hiſtorie to flow ouer, and in the hiſtorie to be shorte. \f 1 \fr 2.33 \ft \+it li. 1. c. 1. v. 1\+it*.\f*
\c 3
\cd When Ieruſalem was in peace, and good order, by the care of Onias high prieſt, and king Seleucus fauoured pious workes, 4. Simon a wicked churchwarden, betrayeth the treaſure of the Sanctuarie: 7. Wherupon Heliodorus ſent by the king, and after curteous intertainment, declaring that he muſt carie al the money to the king, is reſiſted by Onias with others, deuoutly commending the cauſe to God. 23. He neuertheles attempting the ſacrilege, is terrified by a viſion, ſore beaten, and in deſperate miſerie, 31. by Onias ſacrifice and prayers, is reſtored to health: 35. and returning home confeſſeth the powre of God. \f - \ft The firſt part. The perſecution of the Church by Antiochus.\f*
\p
\v 1 THEREFORE \f 1 \fr 3.1 \ft C\f* when the holie \f a \fr 3.1 \ft Three thinges make a comonwealth to be in good ſtate. 1. Good agreement of principal men amongſt themſelues, & with the comonaltie. 2. Exact obſeruation of good lawes: 3. And eminent vertue of the ſupreme gouernour with exerciſe of pietie and hate of ſinnes.\f* citie was inhabited in \f 2 \fr 3.1 \ft 1. Good agreement of principal men amongſt themſelves, & with the commonality.\f* 1. al peace, 2. \f 3 \fr 3.1 \ft 2. Exact obſervation of good laws:\f* the lawes alſo as yet were very wel kept, becauſe of Onias 3. \f 4 \fr 3.1 \ft 3. And eminent virtue of the ſupreme governor with exerciſe of piety and hate of ſins.\f* the high prieſt his pietie, & minde that hated euils,
\v 2 it came to paſſe that kinges alſo themſelues, and princes eſteemed the place worthie of high honour, and glorified the temple with verie great giftes:
\v 3 ſo that Seleucus the king of Aſia of his reuenewes allowed al the charges perteyning to the miniſterie of the ſacrifices.
\v 4 But \f b \fr 3.4 \ft Other three thinges do truble the ſtate: Obſtinacie in offenders, not content to be corrected. 2 Inueterate malice ſeking reuenge againſt iuſt Superiors. 3. and auarice of princes, to robbe the holie or common treaſure.\f* Simon of the tribe of Beniamin, being appointed ouerſeer of the temple, 1 \f 1 \fr 3.4 \ft Obſtinacie in offenders, not content to be corrected.\f* contended, the prince of the prieſtes reſiſting him, to worke ſome wicked thing in the citie.
\v 5 And 2. \f 1 \fr 3.5 \ft 2. Inueterate malice ſeking reuenge againſt juſt Superiors.\f* when he could not ouercome Onias, he came to Appollonius the ſonne of Tharſæas, who at that time was gouernour of Cæleſyria, and Phænicia:
\v 6 and told him, that the treaſurie at Ieruſalem was ful of innumerable deale of money, and the common ſtore to be infinite, which perteyne not to the account of the ſacrifices: and that
\v 7 it is poſſible, al might fal into the kings powre. And 3. \f 1 \fr 3.7 \ft 3. and auarice of princes, to robbe the holie or common treaſure.\f* when Appollonius had made relation to the king, concerning the money that he was told of, he calling for Heliodorus, who was ouer his affayres, ſent him with commiſſion to tranſport the foreſayde money.
\v 8 And forthwith Heliodorus began to take his iourney, in shew in dede as if he would goe viſite the cities through out Cœleſyria and Phænicia, but in very deede to accomplish the kings purpoſe.
\v 9 But when he was come to Ieruſalem, and was curteouſly receiued in the citie by the highprieſt, he told of the the aduertiſment geuen of the money: and opened for what cauſe he was come: and asked if theſe thinges were ſo in very deede.
\v 10 Then the highprieſt shewed that theſe were depoſita, and the liuelihoods of widowes, and pupils:
\v 11 but certaine of them to belong vnto Hircanus of Tobie a very noble man, among theſe thinges, which impious Simon had promoted, & the whole to be of ſiluer foure hundred talents, and of gold two hundred.
\v 12 And that they should be deceiued which had committed it to the place, & the temple, that is honoured through out the whole world, to be a thing, for the reuerence, and holines therof altogether vnpoſſible.
\v 13 But he by reaſon of thoſe thinges, which he had in commiſſion of the king, ſayd in any wiſe that they muſt be caried to the king.
\v 14 And on a day appointed, Heliodorus entered in to take order concerning theſe thinges. But there was noe ſmal trembling through out the whole citie.
\v 15 And the prieſtes caſt them ſelues before the altar with their prieſtes ſtoles, and inuocated him from heauen, which made the law of depoſita, that he would kepe the thinges ſafe, from them that had depoſed them.
\v 16 But now he that ſaw the countenance of the high prieſt, was wounded in minde: for his face and colour being changed declared the inner ſorow of the minde.
\v 17 For there was a certaine penſiuenes powred about the man, and horrour of the bodie, wherby the ſorow of his hart was made manifeſt to them that beheld him.
\v 18 Others alſo came flocking together out of their houſes: praying with publicke ſupplication, for that the place was to come into contempt.
\v 19 And the wemen hauing their breaſt girded with heareclothes, came together through the ſtretes. Yea and the virgins \f c \fr 3.19 \ft Theſe virgins remained in places nere to the temple, brought vp in exerciſes of pietie, faſting and praying, til they were deſpouſed. \+it 1. Reg. 2. v. 22. S. Amb. li. 1. de virgint. S. Greg. Niſſen: Orat. de Chriſti. Natiuit. S. Damas. li. 4. c. 13\+it*.\f* that were shut vp, came forth to Onias, and ſome to the walles, but ſome looked through the windowes:
\v 20 and al ſtretching forth their handes vnto heauen, prayed.
\v 21 For the expectation of the confuſe multitude, and of the grand prieſt being in an agonie, was miſerable.
\v 22 And theſe certes inuocated almightie God, that the thinges committed to them, might be performed with al integritie for them that had committed the ſame of truſt.
\v 23 But Heliodorus exerciſed that which he had decreed in the ſame place himſelf preſent with his gard about the treaſurie.
\v 24 But the ſpirit of almightie God made great euidence of his appearing, ſo that al which had preſumed to obey him, falling by the vertue of God, were turned into diſſolution and feare.
\v 25 For \f d \fr 3.25 \ft \+it 4. Reg. 2. v. 11\+it*. A firie chariotte and firie horſes caried Elias from \+it Elizeus. 4. Reg. 6. v. 17\+it*. And the mountaine appeared ful of horſes, and of firie chariots round about \+it Elizeus\+it*, no leſſe ſtrange then this viſion. See S. Ambroſe \+it li. 2. c. 29. Offic\+it*. diſcourſing vpon this hiſtorie.\f* there appeared to them a certaine horſe hauing a terrible rider, adorned with very rich harneſſe: and he with feircenes ſtroke Heliodorus with his forefoote, and he that ſate vpon him, ſeemed to haue armour of gold.
\v 26 There appeared alſo two other yong men comely for ſtrength, excellent of glorie, and beautiful in attyre: which ſtood about him, and on both ſides whipped him, beating him with manie ſtripes without intermiſſion.
\v 27 And Heliodorus ſodenly fel on the ground, & they tooke him being couered round about with much darkenes, and being ſet in a ſeate portatiue, they thruſt him forth.
\v 28 And he that with manie currers, and men of his garde entered into the foreſayde treaſurie, was caried no man geuing him ſuccourſe, the manifeſt powre of God being knowne.
\v 29 And he in deede by the powre of God lay dumbe, and depriued of al hope, and health.
\v 30 But they bleſſed our Lord, becauſe he magnified his place: and the temple, that a little before was ful of feare and tumult, almightie God appearing, was filled with ioy and gladnes.
\v 31 And then certaine of the freindes of Heliodorus forthwith deſired Onias, that he would inuocate the higheſt to geue him life, who was at the very laſt gaſpe.
\v 32 And the highprieſt conſidering leaſt perhaps the king might ſuſpect ſome malice on the Iewes part downe aboute Heliodorns, offered for the health of the man an healthful hoſt.
\v 33 And when the highprieſt by prayer obteyned, the ſelfe ſame yong men, clothed with the ſame garments, ſtanding by Heliodorus, ſayd: Geue thankes to Onias the prieſt: for him hath our Lord geuen thee life.
\v 34 But thou being ſcourged of God, declare vnto al men the great workes and the powre of God. And theſe thinges being ſayd, they appeared no more.
\v 35 And Heliodorus hauing offered an hoſt to God, and hauing promiſed great vowes to him, that granted him to liue, and geuing thankes to Onias, taking his armie againe returned to the king.
\v 36 And he teſtified to al men thoſe workes of the great God, which he had ſeene before his owne eyes.
\v 37 And when the king had asked Heliodorus; who was meete to be ſent yet once more to Ieruſalem, he ſayd:
\v 38 If thou haue anie enemie, or traytour to thy kingdome, ſend him thither, and thou shalt receiue him againe ſcourged, if yet he eſcape: becauſe there is vndoubtedly in the place a certaine powre of God.
\v 39 For he that hath his dwelling in the heauens, is the viſiter, and helper of that place, & them that come to doe euil, he ſtriketh, and deſtroyeth.
\v 40 Therefore concerning Heliodorus, and the keping of the treaſurie ſo the matter ſtandeth.
\c 4
\cd Onias defamed & moleſted by Simon, repayreth to the king to procure peace. 7. Seleucus dying, & Antiochus reigning, Iaſon by promiſing money to the king, getteth the office of highprieſt from his brother Onias: 10. and peruerteth religion: 12. ſetteth vp a wicked ſchoole, wherby manie are corrupted: 8. ſendeth money for ſacrifice to be offered to Hercules, which is imployed in making gallees. 21. Antiochus is receiued with great pompe into Ieruſalem. 23. Menelaus by promiſe of more money getteth the high prieſthood from Iaſon. 29. He alſo is depoſed, and his brother Liſimachus put in the place. 32. Andronicus trayterouſly murdereth Onias. 36. and for the ſame is ſlaine by the kings commandment: 39. and Lyſimachus by the people. 43. Menelaus iuſtly accuſed eſcapeth by bribing, and the innocent are ſlaine.
\p
\v 1 BVT Simon the foreſayde betrayer of the money, and of his countrie, \f a \fr 4.1 \ft It is the common practiſe of al traytors to calumniate and defame good gouerners.\f* ſpake il of Onias, as though he had ſtirred vp Heliodorus to theſe thinges, and had bene the mouer of the euils:
\v 2 and the prouider for the citie, and defender of his nation, and the emulatour of the law of God, he preſumed to cal a ſecret betrayer of the kingdom.
\v 3 But when the emnities proceeded ſo far, that murders alſo were committed by certaine of Simons familiars:
\v 4 Onias conſidering the peril of the contention, and that Appollonius being gouernour of Cæleſyria, and Phænicia, was outragious, which encreaſed the malice of Simon, \f b \fr 4.4 \ft And the beſt remedie againſt ſuch ſeducers is by auctoritie of Superior povvre, not by the people, who are comonly more prone to fauour faction then inſtice.\f* went to the king,
\v 5 not as an accuſer of the citizens, but conſidering with himſelf the common profite of the whole multitude.
\v 6 For he ſaw that without the kings prouidence it was vnpoſſible that peace should be made in thoſe matters, & that Simon would not ceaſe from his follie. \f 1 \fr 4.6 \ft \+it li. 1. c. 1. v. 11\+it*.\f*
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 4.7 \ft E\f* But after Seleucus departure out of life, when Antiochus that was called the Noble, had taken the kingdom vpon him, Iaſon the brother of Onias ambiciouſly ſought the highprieſthood:
\v 8 going to the king, promiſing him three hundred three ſcore talents of ſiluer, and of other reuenewes foure ſcore talents,
\v 9 aboue this he promiſed alſo an hundred fiftie more, if leaue might be granted him to make a ſchole, and a place for youth, and to intitle them, that were at Ieruſalem, Antiochians.
\v 10 Which when the king had granted, and he had obteyned the princedom, forthwith he began to tranſport his countrie men to the heathen rite.
\v 11 And theſe thinges being taken away, which of fauoure had bene decreed by kinges vnto the Iewes, and through Iohn the father of the Eupolemus, who was embaſſadour with the Romans concerning amitie and ſocietie, he diſanulling the lawful rites of the citizens, made wicked ordinances.
\v 12 For he preſumed vnder the verie caſtle to ſet vp a ſchoole, and to put al the goodlieſt youthes in brothel houſes.
\v 13 And this was not the beginning, but a certaine increaſe, and going forward of the heathen and ſtrange conuerſation, through the abhominable neuer heard before, of Iaſon the impious and not a prieſt.
\v 14 So that the prieſtes were not now occupied about the offices of the altar, but \f c \fr 4.14 \ft Where true Religion is abolished, moſt men neglect al shew of religion, and rather applie themſelues to vanities, or worſe ſinnes.\f* the temple being contemned, & the ſacrifices neglected, they haſtened to be pertakers of the gamme of wraſtling, and of the vniuſt maintenance therof, and in the exerciſe of the coyte.
\v 15 And ſetting nought by the honours of their fathers, they eſteemed the Greeke glories for the beſt:
\v 16 by reaſon wherof they had dangerous contention, and they had emulation toward their ordinances, and in al thinges they coueted to be like to them, whom they had enemies and murderers.
\v 17 For to doe impiouſly againſt the lawes of God eſcapeth not vnpunished, but this the time folowing wil declare.
\v 18 And when the gamme vſed euerie fifth yeare was kept at Tyre, and the king was preſent,
\v 19 the wicked Iaſon ſent from Ieruſalem ſinful men, carying three hundred didrachmaes of ſiluer for the ſacrifice of Hercules, which they that caryed it, requeſted that it might not be beſtowed on the ſacrifices, becauſe it ought not, but that it might be deputed for other charges.
\v 20 And theſe were offered in dede by him that ſent them, vnto the ſacrifice of Hercules: but becauſe of them that were preſent they were geuen to the making of gallees. \f 1 \fr 4.20 \ft \+it li. 1. c. 1. v. 17\+it*.\f*
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 4.21 \ft G\f* And Apollonius the ſonne of Mneſtheus being ſent into Ægypt becauſe of the nobles of Ptolomee Philometor the king, \f d \fr 4.21 \ft This king falſly pretended to reſtore his kinſman (his ſiſters, ſonne) to the kingdom being depriued by his younger brother \+it (Liuius li. 44\+it*) but the true cauſe of his ſending Apolonius into Ægypt, was to ſubdue that kingdom to himſelf. \+it 1. Mach. 1. v. 17\+it*.\f* when Antiochus vnderſtood that himſelf was made an alien from the affaires of the kingdom, prouiding for his owne commodities, departing thence he came to Ioppe, and from thence to Ieruſalem.
\v 22 And being magnifically receiued of Iaſon, and the citie, entered in with torch lights, and with prayſes: and from thence he turned his armie into Phænicia.
\v 23 And after the time of three yeares Iaſon ſent Menelaus brother of the foreſaide Simon, carying money to the king, and to bring anſwers of neceſſarie affayres.
\v 24 But he being commended to the king, when he had magnified the preſence of his power, wreſted the high prieſthood vpon him ſelf, ouer bidding Iaſon three hundred talents of ſiluer.
\v 25 And hauing receiued commiſſion from the king, he came, hauing in deede nothing worthie of the prieſthood: but bearing the mind of a cruel tyrant, and the wrath of a wilde beaſt.
\v 26 And Iaſon indeede who had circumuented his owne brother, being himſelf deceiued was driuen out a fugitiue into the countrie of the Ammanites.
\v 27 And \f e \fr 4.27 \ft Menelaus brother of Simon \+it (v. 23\+it*) of the tribe of Beniamin \+it (ch. 3. v. 4\+it*.) was not by the law capable of the prieſthood, which only perteined to the progenie of Aaron of the tribe of Leui. So in this trubleſome time the right ſucceſſion of highprieſtes was intermitted, and reſtored in Mathathias. \+it li. 1. c. 2\+it*.\f* Menelaus obteyned the princedom: but concerning the money promiſed to the king he did nothing, wheras Soſtratus that was gouernour of the caſtel exacted it.
\v 28 For to him perteyned the exacting of the tributes: for which cauſe they were both called out to the king.
\v 29 And Menelaus was remoued from the prieſthood, Lyſimachus his brother ſucceeding: and Soſtratus was made gouernour of the Cyprians.
\v 30 And when theſe things were a doing, it chanced the Tharſians, and the Mallotians to moue ſedition, becauſe they were geuen for a gift to king Antiochus concubine.
\v 31 The king therfore came in haſt to pacifie them, one of his companions Adronicus being lefte ſubſtitute.
\v 32 But Menelaus ſuppoſing that he had taken a conuenient time, ſtealing certaine veſſels of gold out of the temple, gaue them to Adronicus, and others he had ſould at Tyre, & in their neere cities:
\v 33 Which thing when Onias vnderſtood moſt certainly, he rebuked him, keeping himſelf in a ſafe place at Antioche beſide Daphne.
\v 34 Wherupon Menelaus coming to Andronicus, deſired him to kil Onias. Who when he was come to Onias, and right handes being geuen with an oath (although he was ſuſpected of him) had perſwaded him to come out of the ſanctuarie, immediatly he ſlew him, not reuerencing iuſtice.
\v 35 For which cauſe not only the Iewes, but alſo other nations likewiſe were offended, and tooke it greuouſly for the vniuſt murder of ſo great a man.
\v 36 But when the king was returned out of the places of Cilicia, the Iewes went vnto him at Antioch, and alſo \f f \fr 4.36 \ft True and ſolide vertue moued the common people to compaſſion, the King himſelf to teares, the Tyrians to honour the bodies of the innocent with coſtlie burial. \+it v. 49\+it*.\f* the Greekes: complayning of the vniuſt murder of Onias.
\v 37 Antiochus therefore was ſorie in his minde for Onias, and being inclined to pitie, he shed teares, remembring the ſobrietie and modeſtie of the deceaſed.
\v 38 And his hart being incenſed, he commanded Andronicus being ſpoiled of the purple, to be led about al the citie: and that in the ſame place, wherin he had committed the impiety vpon Onias, the ſacrilegious perſon should be depriued of his life, our Lord repaying him worthie punishment.
\v 39 And manie ſacrilegies being committed of Lyſimachus in the temple by the counſel of Menelaus, and the rumour being bruited abrode, the multitude gathered together againſt Lyſimachus, much gold being now caryed out.
\v 40 But the multitudes making inſurrection, and their mindes replenished with anger, Lyſimachus arming almoſt three thouſand began to vſe vniuſt handes, a certaine tyrant being captaine farre growne in age, and alſo in madnes.
\v 41 But as they vnderſtood the endeuour of Lyſimachus, ſome tooke ſtones, ſome ſtrong clubbles: and certaine threw ashes.
\v 42 And manie in deede were wounded, & certaine alſo throwne to the ground, but al were put to flight: the ſacrilegious perſon alſo himſelf they ſlew beſide the treaſurie.
\v 43 Concerning theſe thinges therefore iudgement began to be commenced againſt Menelaus.
\v 44 And when the king was come to Tyre, three men ſent from the ancients, put vp the matter vnto him.
\v 45 And when Menelaus was ouercome, he promiſed Ptolomee to geue much money to perſwade the king.
\v 46 Ptolomee therfore went to the king being in a certaine courte, as it were to coole himſelf, and brought him from his purpoſe:
\v 47 and Menelaus certes being guiltie of al the euil was quitted of the crimes: and the poore wretches, who if they had pleaded the cauſe euen before Scythians should be iudged innocent, them he commanded to death.
\v 48 Quickly then did they vniuſtly ſuffer, which proſecuted the cauſe for the citie, & the people, and the ſacred veſſels.
\v 49 For the which thing the Tyrians alſo being offended, were very liberal towards the burial of them.
\v 50 But Menelaus becauſe of their auarice that were in power, continewed in authoritie, increaſing in malice to the betraying of the citizens.
\c 5
\cd Viſions of armies fighting in the ayre appeare in Ieruſalem fourtie dayes. 5. Iaſon with a thouſand men ſurpriſeth the citie, killeth manie citizens, but is expulſed and dyeth myſerably. 11. Antiochus perſecuteth the Iewes, 15. ſpoyleth the temple, and prophaneth holie thinges. 27. Iudas with others flee into the deſert.
\p
\v 1 AT the ſame time Antiochus prepared a ſecond iourney into Ægypt.
\v 2 And it came to paſſe: that through out the whole citie of Ieruſalem were \f a \fr 5.2 \ft Strange thinges aboue the ordinarie courſe of nature doe euer ſignifie Gods wrath, for mens tranſgreſſion, and are admonitions to turne from ſinne, with ſpede that we may eſcape the heauie hand of Gods iuſtice. So the Emperour Charles the Great interpreted the apparition of a great Comete as witneſſeth \+it Faſciculus temporum\+it*.\f* ſenne for fourtie dayes in the ayre horſemen running hither & thither, hauing golden ſtoles, and ſpeares, as it were companies armed,
\v 3 and courſing of horſes ſet in orders by rankes, and that there were encounterings together neere hand, and shakings of sheildes, and a multitude of men in helmets with ſwordes drawen, and throwing of darts, and the glittering of golden armour, and of al kind of harnes.
\v 4 Wherefore al prayed that the wonders might be turned to good.
\v 5 But when there was a falſe rumour gone forth, as though Antiochus had bene parted this life, Iaſon taking vnto him no leſſe then a thouſand men, ſuddenly ſet vpon the citie: and the citizens flying together to the wal, at the laſt the citie being taken, Menelaus fled into the caſtel.
\v 6 But Iaſon ſpared not his citizens in murder, nor conſidered, that proſperitie againſt kinſmen is a verie great euil, ſuppoſing that he should take the victorious ſpoiles of the enemies, and not of his citizens.
\v 7 And the princedome verily he obteyned not, but receiued confuſion, the end of his treacherie, and went againe a fugitiue into the countrie of the Ammanites.
\v 8 At the laſt to his owne deſtruction being incloſed of Aretas the tyrant of the Arabians, flying from citie to citie, odious to al men, as an apoſtata from the lawes, and execrable, as an enemie of his countrie and citizens, he was thruſt out into Egypt:
\v 9 and he that had expelled manie out of their countrie, perished in a ſtrange place, going to the Lacedemonians, as being like \f b \fr 5.9 \ft Lacedemonians otherwiſe called Spartians, deſcended from the ſtocke of Abraham. \+it li. 1. c. 12. v. 2\+it*.\f* for kindred ſake to haue refuge there:
\v 10 but he that caſt away manie vnburyed, himſelfe both vnlamented, and vnburyed is caſt forth, neither enioying forrein buryal, nor partaker of the ſepulcher of his fathers . \f 1 \fr 5.10 \ft \+it li. 1. c. 1. v. 21\+it*.\f*
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 5.11 \ft I\f* Theſe thinges therefore being done, the king ſuſpected that the Iewes would forſake the ſocietie: and for this departing out of Ægypt with a furious mind, he tooke the citie by armes.
\v 12 And he bad the ſouldiars kil, and not ſpare them that came in their way, to murder them that went vp into the houſes.
\v 13 Slaughters therfore were made of youngmen & old, and deſtructions of wemen and children, and murders of virgins and litle ones.
\v 14 And there were in the whole three dayes foure ſcore thouſand ſlaine, fourtie thouſand priſoners, and no leſſe ſold.
\v 15 But neither do theſe thinges ſuffice, he preſumed alſo to enter into the temple, in al the earth the moſt holie, Menelaus being his leader, who was betrayer of the lawes, and his countrie.
\v 16 And with wicked handes taking the holie veſſels, which by other kinges and cities were ſet for the ornament and the glorie of the place, he vnworthily handled and contaminated them.
\v 17 So Antiochus being alienated in minde, conſidered not, that for the ſinnes of them that inhabit the citie, God had bene angrie a litle: for the which alſo hapned the contempte about the place:
\v 18 \x - \xt ch. 3. v. 23. 27.\x* otherwiſe vnles it had chanced them to haue bene wrapped in manie ſinnes, as Heliodorus, who was ſent of Seleucus the king to ſpoile the treaſurie, this man alſo immediately as he came had bene ſcourged, and repelled verily from his boldnes.
\v 19 But \f c \fr 5.19 \ft Al rites of religion with temples and other holie thinges are ordayned to the Seruice of God, and for mens ſpiritual good, and therfore when men ceaſe to ſerue God, as holie thinges are deſtroyed, or taken away.\f* not the nation for the place, but the place for the nation hath God choſen.
\v 20 And therefore the place alſo it ſelf is made partaker of the peoples euils: but afterward it shal be partaker of the good thinges, and it that was forſaken in the wrath of almightie God, shal be exalted againe with great glorie in the reconciliation of the great Lord.
\v 21 Therefore Antiochus hauing taken away out of the temple a thouſand and eight hundred talents, ſpedily went backe to Antioch, thinking through pride, that he might bring the land to ſayle vpon, & the ſea to goe vpon, through haughtines of minde.
\v 22 And he left alſo rulers to afflict the nation: at Ieruſalem, Philip a Phrygian borne, more cruel of maners then he himſelf by whom he was appointed:
\v 23 and in Garizim Andronicus and Menelaus, who lay more greiuouſly vpon the citizens then the reſt.
\v 24 And wheras he was ſet againſt the Iewes, he ſent the odious prince Apollonius with an armie of two & twentie thouſand, commanding him to kil al of perfect age, to ſel the wemen and the young ones.
\v 25 Who when he was come to Ieruſalem, feyning peace, reſted vntil the holie day of the Sabbath: and then the Iewes keping holie day, he commanded his men to take weapons.
\v 26 And he murdered al that were gone forth to behold the gammes: & running through the citie with armed men he ſlew a very greate multitude.
\v 27 But Iudas Machabeus, who was \f d \fr 5.27 \ft Iudas was the tenth lawful highprieſt from the Monarchie of the Grecians.\f* the tenth, was retyred into a deſert place, and there amongſt wilde beaſtes he led his life in the mountaines with his companie: and they abode eating meate of graſſe, that they might not be partakers of the contamination.
\c 6
\cd The law of God is abolished, the temple prophaned, and named of Iupiter Olympius. 7. The feaſt of Bacchus is kept. 10. wemen with their circumciſed children are ſlaine. 11. others for celebrating the ſabbath (12. an admonition to the reader) 18. old Eleazarus conſtantly obſeruing the law ſuffereth glorious death.
\p
\v 1 BVT not long after the king ſent a certaine ancient man of Antioch, that should compel the Iewes to remoue themſelues from the lawes of their fathers and of God:
\v 2 to contaminate alſo the temple that was in Ieruſalem, and to cal it by the name of Iupiter Olympius: and in \f a \fr 6.2 \ft Sanaballat in the time of Alexander the great procured a temple to be built in Garizim like to that in Ieruſalem. An other was built in Ægypt by Ananias in in the time of Ptolomee Philometor both ſchiſmatical. \+it Ioſephus. l. 11. c. 8. & li. 13. c. 6\+it*.\f* Garizim, according as they were that inhabited the place, of Iupiter Hoſpitalis.
\v 3 And the inuaſion of the euiles was ſore and grieucus to al:
\v 4 for the temple was ful of the lecherie and glottonie of the Gentiles: & of them that played the harlots with whoores. And wemen thruſting themſelues of their owne accord into the ſacred houſes, bringing in thouſe thinges which were not lawful.
\v 5 The altar alſo was ful of vnlawful thinges, which were forbidden by the lawes.
\v 6 And neither were the Sabbaths kept, nor the ſoleme dayes of the fathers obſerued neither plainely did anie man confeſſe him ſelfe to be a Iewe.
\v 7 But they were led with bitter neceſſitie in the kings birth day to ſacrifices: and when the feaſt of Bacchus was kept, they were compelled to goe about crowned with Iuie vnto Bacchus.
\v 8 And there went forth a decree into the next cities of the Gentiles, the Ptolomeans geuing the aduiſe, that they alſo in like manner should doe againſt the Iewes, that they might ſacrifice:
\v 9 and them that would not paſſe to the ordinances of the heathen, \f b \fr 6.9 \ft Beſides former great maſſaker, \+it c. 5.\+it* foure moſt notorious martyrdomes are here related. 1. Wemen with their circumciſed children. 2. Other people for keeping the ſabbath.\f* they should kil. A man then might ſee the miſerie.
\v 10 For 1. \f 1 \fr 6.10 \ft 1. Women with their circumciſed children.\f* two wemen were accuſed to haue circuncided their children: whom, the infantes hanging at their breaſts, when they had openly led them about through the citie, they threwe downe headlong by the walles.
\v 11 And others coming together to the next caues, & ſecretely \f 1 \fr 6.11 \ft 2. Other people for keeping the ſabbath.\f* 2. keping the day of the Sabbath, when they were diſcouered to Philip, were burnt with fyre, becauſe they feared for religion and obſeruance, to helpe themſelues with their hand.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 6.12 \ft A neceſſarie admonition to the weake in time of perſecution.\f* But I beſeech them that shal read this booke, that they abhorre not for the aduerſities, but that they account thoſe thinges, which haue happened, not to be for the deſtruction, but for the chaſtening of our ſtocke.
\v 13 For not to ſuffer ſinners a long time to doe as they wil, but forthwith to punish, is a token of a great benefite.
\v 14 For, not as in other nations our Lord patiently expecteth, that when the day of iudgement shal come, he may punish them in the fulnes of ſinnes:
\v 15 ſo alſo doth he determine in vs, that our ſinnes being come to the end, ſo at length he may punish vs.
\v 16 For which cauſe he neuer certes remoueth away his mercie from vs: but chaſtening his people by aduerſitie, he forſaketh them not.
\v 17 But let theſe thinges be ſayd of vs in few wordes for an admonition of the readers. And now we muſt come to the ſtorie.
\v 18 Therfore 3. \f 1 \fr 6.18 \ft 3. Eleazarus nintie yeares old cruelly ſlaine.\f* Eleazarus one \f c \fr 6.18 \ft He was excellently lerned in holie Scriptures and in al diuine and humane knowlege.\f* of the chief of the Scribes, a man ſtriken in age, and comely of countenance, with open mouth gaping was compelled to eate ſwines flesh.
\v 19 But he embracing rather a moſt glorious death then an hateful life, went before voluntarily to the punishment.
\v 20 And conſidering how he ought to come patiently ſuſteyning, he determined not to committe vnlawful thinges for loue of life.
\v 21 But they that ſtood by, moued with vnlawful pitie, for the old frendshipe of the man, taking him in ſecrete, deſired that flesh might be brought, which it was lawful for him to eate, that he \f d \fr 6.21 \ft To feyne or make outward shew of conſent to falſe religion, is neuer lawful.\f* might feyne to haue eaten, as the king had commanded,
\v 22 of the flesh of the ſacrifice: that by this fact he might be deliuered from death: and for the old freindshipe of the man, they did him this courteſie.
\v 23 But he begane to thinke vpon the worthie preeminence of his age and ancientnes, and the houre heares of natural nobilitie, & his doinges from a childe of very good conuerſation, and according to the ordinances, and the holie law made of God, he anſwered quickly, ſaying: that he would rather \f e \fr 6.23 \ft In the old teſtament none could enter into heauen, but the moſt iuſt went to \+it Limbus\+it* when they died.\f* be ſent vnto hel.
\v 24 For it is not meete, quoth he, for our \f f \fr 6.24 \ft Old age (ſaith S. Ambroſe \+it li. 2 c. 10 de Iacob patri\+it*.) ought to be the hauen, not the ſhipvvrake of thy former life.\f* age to feyne: that manie young men thinking, that Eleazarus of foure ſcore yeare & tenne is paſſed to the life of Aliens:
\v 25 they alſo through my diſſimulation, and for a litle time of corruptible life, may be deceiued, and hereby I may purchaſe a ſtayne, and a curſe to mine old age.
\v 26 For although at this preſent time I be deliuered from the punishments of men, yet neither aliue nor dead shal I eſcape the hand of the Almightie.
\v 27 Wherfore in departing manfully out of this life, I shal appeare worthie of mine old age:
\v 28 and to yong men I shal leaue a conſtant example, if with readie mind and ſtoutly I ſuffer an honeſt death, for the moſt graue and moſt holie lawes. Theſe thinges being ſpoken, forthwith he was drawen to execution.
\v 29 And they that led him, and had bene a litle before more milde, were turned into wrath for the wordes ſpoken of him, which they thought were vttered through arrogancie.
\v 30 But when he was now in killing with the ſtrokes, he groned, and ſayd: O Lord, which haſt the holie knowlege, thou knoweſt manifeſtly that wheras I might be deliuered from death, I doe ſuſteyne ſore paines of the bodie: but according to the ſoule, for thy feare I doe willingly ſuffer theſe thinges.
\v 31 And this man certes in this maner departed this life, leauing not only to yong men, but alſo to the whole nation the memorie of his death for an example of vertue and fortitude.
\c 7
\cd The noble Martyrdome of ſeuen bretheren, refuſing to eate ſwines flesh: and boldly admonishing king Antiochus of his damnable ſtate. 41. Laſtly the mother (hauing encoreged her ſonnes) likewiſe dyeth gloriouſly.
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 7.1 \ft Whoſoeuer pleaſe to read more of theſe glorious Martyres, may ſee the large diſcourſes of Flauius Ioſephus in his booke \+it de Machabeis\+it*, And of ſundrie ancient Fathers: S. Cyprian. \+it li. 4. Epiſt. ep. 6\+it*. S. Chryſoſtom, \+it homilia de natiuitate ſeptem Machabæorum\+it*. S. Ambroſe. \+it li. 1. Offic. c. 40. &c. li. 11. de Iacob. c. 9\+it*. S. Auguſtin, \+it de origine anima. Tract. 8. in Epiſt. 1. Ioan. & Ser. 110. de diuerſis\+it*. S. Proſper: \+it li. de prædict. par 2. c. 40\+it*. S. Prudentius. \+it hymno de Romano Martyre\+it*. S. Leo. \+it Ser. de Nat ſeptem fratrum. Machab\+it*. S. Gaudentius. \+it Brixanus Tractatu de Machabeis\+it*. S. Ephrem. \+it Ser. de morte\+it*. S. Victorinus A ſer. \+it Carmine de ſeptem Machabeis\+it*.\f* it came to paſſe, 4. \f 2 \fr 7.1 \ft The fourth Martyrdom was of ſeuen bretheren and their mother.\f* that ſeuen brethren together with their mother being apprehended, to be compelled by the king to eate againſt the law ſwines flesh, were tormented with whippes and ſcourges.
\v 2 But one of them which was the firſt, ſayd thus: What ſeekeſt thou, and what wilt thou lerne of vs? we are readie to dye rather then to tranſgreſſe the lawes of God, coming from our fathers.
\v 3 The king therfore being wrath commanded frying pannes, and braſen pottes to be heated:
\v 4 the which forthwith being heated, he commanded his tongue, that had ſpoken firſt, to be cut out: and the skinne of his head being drawen of, the endes alſo of his handes and feete to be chopped of, the reſt of his bretheren, and his mother looking on.
\v 5 And when he was now made in al partes vnprofitable, he commanded fire to be put vnto him, and that breathing as yet he should be fried in the frying panne: wherin when he was long tormented, the reſt together with the mother exhorted one an other to dye manfully,
\v 6 ſaying: Our Lord God wil behold the truth, and wil take pleaſure in vs, as Moyſes declared in the profeſſion of the Canticle: \x - \xt Deut. 32. v. 43.\x* And in his ſeruants he wil take pleaſure.
\v 7 That firſt therfore being dead in this maner, they brought the next to make him a mocking ſtocke: & the skinne of his head with the heares being drawen of, they asked if he would eate, before that he were punished throughout the whole bodie in euerie member.
\v 8 But he anſwering in his countrie ſpeach, ſaid: I wil not doe it. Wherfore this alſo in the next place, receiued the torments of the firſt:
\v 9 and being at the verie laſt gaſpe, thus he ſaid: Thou in dede ô moſt wicked man in this preſent life deſtroyeſt vs: but the king of the world wil raiſe vs vp which dye for his lawes, in the reſurrection of eternal life.
\v 10 After him the third is had in deriſion, and being demanded his tongue, he quickly put it forth, and conſtantly ſtretched out his handes:
\v 11 and with confidence he ſaid: From heauen doe I poſſeſſe theſe, but for the lawes of God now doe I contemne theſe ſelfe ſame, becauſe I hope that I shal receiue them againe of him.
\v 12 So that the king, and they that were with him merueled at the yong mans courege, becauſe he eſtemed the torments as nothing.
\v 13 And this being thus dead, the fourth they vexed in like maner tormenting him.
\v 14 And when he was now euen to dye, thus he ſaid: It is better for them that are put to death by men to exſpect hope of God, that they shal be rayſed vp againe by him. For to thee there shal not be reſurrection vnto life.
\v 15 And when they had brought the fifth, they tormented him. But he looking vpon him,
\v 16 ſayd: Thou hauing power among men, wheras thou art corruptible doeſt what thou wilt: but thinke not that our ſtock is forſaken of God.
\v 17 And doe thou patiently abide, and thou shalt ſee his great power, in what ſort he wil torment thee, and thy ſeede.
\v 18 After him they brought the ſixth, and he beginning to dye, ſayd thus: Be not deceiued vainely: for we ſuffer this for our owne ſakes, ſinning againſt our God, and thinges worthie of admiration are done in vs:
\v 19 but doe not thinke that thou shalt eſcape vnpunished, for that thou haſt attempted to fight againſt God.
\v 20 But the mother aboue meaſure meruelous, and worthie of good mens memorie, which beholding her ſeuen ſonnes perishing in one dayes ſpace, bare it with a good hart, for the hope that she had in God:
\v 21 exhorted euerie one of them in their countrie language manfully, being replenished with wiſedome: and ioyning a mans hart to a womans cogitation,
\v 22 she ſayd to them: I know not how you appeared in my wombe: for neither did I geue you ſpirit and ſoule and life, and the members of euerie one I my ſelfe framed not.
\v 23 But in deede the Creator of the world, that hath formed the natiuitie of man, and that inuented the origine of al, & he wil reſtore againe with mercie vnto you ſpirit and life, as now you deſpiſe your ſelues for his lawes.
\v 24 But Antiochus thinking himſelfe contemned, and withal deſpiſing the voice of the vpbrayder, when the yonger was yet aliue, he did not only exhort with wordes, but alſo with oth he affirmed that he would make him rich & happie, and being turned from the lawes of his fathers, he would account him a freind, and geue him thinges neceſſarie.
\v 25 But when the yong man was not inclined to theſe thinges, the king called the mother, and counſelled her to deale with the yong man to ſaue his life.
\v 26 And when he had exhorted her in manie wordes, \f a \fr 7.26 \ft A promiſe is properly of a good thing, & bindeth the promiſer to do that vvhich is in dede good. In ſo much that whoſoeuer promiſeth, ſweareth, or voweth to do euil, is bond not to do it. And to do it, is a diſtinct ſinne beſides the former.\f* she promiſed that she would counſel her ſonne.
\v 27 Therfore bending towards him, \f b \fr 7.27 \ft In that this godlie woman deceiued the tyrant, ſhe did vvel, lavvfully vſing æquiuocation.\f* mocking the cruel tyrant, she ſayd in her countrie language: My ſonne haue pitie on me, which haue borne thee in my wombe nine moneths, and gaue thee milke for three yeares, and nourished thee, and brought thee vnto this age.
\v 28 I beſeech thee my ſonne, that thou looke to heauen and earth, and to al thinges that are in them: and vnderſtand that God of nothing made them and mankinde:
\v 29 ſo shal it come to paſſe, that thou wilt not feare this tormenter but being made a worthie partaker with thy bretheren, take thou death, that in that mercie I may receiue thee againe with thy bretheren.
\v 30 When she as yet was ſaying theſe thinges, the yong man ſayd: For whom ſtay you? I obey not the commandement of the king, but the commandement of the law, which was geuen vs by Moyſes.
\v 31 But thou that art become the inuenter of al malice againſt the Hebrewes, shal not eſcape the hand of God.
\v 32 For we for our ſinnes doe ſuffer theſe thinges.
\v 33 And if the Lord our God hath bene angrie with vs a litle for rebuke & correction: yet he wil be reconciled againe to his ſeruants.
\v 34 But thou ô wicked, and of al men moſt flagicious, be not in vaine extolled with vaine hopes, inflamed againſt his ſeruantes.
\v 35 For thou haſt not yet eſcaped the iudgement of the almightie God, and him that beholdeth al thinges.
\v 36 For my bretheren hauing now ſuſteyned short payne, are become vnder the teſtament of eternal life: but thou by the iudgement of God shalt receiue iuſt punishment for thy pride.
\v 37 And I as alſo my brethren doe yeld my life and my bodie for the lawes of our fathers: inuocating God to be propicious to our nation quickly, and that thou with torments and ſtripes maiſt confeſſe that he onlie is God.
\v 38 But in me and in my brethren shal the wrath of the Almightie ceaſe, which hath iuſtly bene brought vpon al our ſtocke.
\v 39 Then the king incenſed with anger, raged againſt him more cruelly aboue al the reſt, taking it grieuouſly that he was mocked.
\v 40 And this ſame therfore died vnſpotted, wholy truſting in our Lord.
\v 41 And laſt of al after the ſonnes the mother alſo was conſumed.
\v 42 Therfore of the ſacrifices, and of the exceeding cruelties there is ynough ſayd. \f 1 \fr 7.42 \ft \+it li. 1. c. 2. v. 1\+it*.\f*
\c 8
\cd Iudas Machabeus with ſix thouſand men, commending their cauſe to God, 6. proſpereth in battel. 8. Philippe ſoliciting for more helpe, Nicanor and Gorgias are ſent with twentie thouſand men againſt Iudas. 12. whoſe men beginning to feare, and ſome flying he encoregeth the reſt, 19. reciting manie examples of Gods aſſiſtance: 22. diſpoſeth his armie, and preuayleth. 30. killing manie of Timothees and Bacchides men. 34. The principal hardly eſcaping by flight, acknowlege that God protecteth the Iewes. \f - \ft The ſecond part of the hiſtory. The wars of the Machabees begun by Mathathias. and proſecuted by Judas.\f*
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 8.1 \ft L\f* Iudas Machabeus and they that were with him, went in ſecretely into the townes: and calling together their kinſemen and taking vnto them thoſe that continewed in Iudaiſme, they brought out to them ſix thouſand men.
\v 2 And \f a \fr 8.2 \ft In al good attemptes deuout prayer is the firſt preparation. And no where more neceſſary then in battel. As wel for good ſucceſſe (ſuppoſing alwayes a good cauſe) as alſo that euerie one pray for his owne ſoule, that it be in ſtate of grace.\f* they inuocated our Lord, that he would haue reſpect vnto his people that was troden of al, and would haue mercie on the temple, that was contaminated of the impious:
\v 3 he would haue pitie alſo vpon the deſtruction of the citie, which was forthwith to be made flat with the ground, and would heare the voice of the bloud crying to him:
\v 4 he would remember alſo the moſt vniuſt deathes of innocent children, and the blaſphemies done to his name, and would take indignation for them.
\v 5 But Machabeus hauing gathered a multitude, became intolerable to the heathen: for the wrath of our Lord was turned into mercie.
\v 6 And coming vpon the caſtels, and cities vnlooked for, he burnt them: & taking commodious places, he made not few ſlaughters of the enemies:
\v 7 and eſpecially in the nightes he was caried to ſuch excurſions, and the fame of his manlines was ſpred abrode euerie where. \f 1 \fr 8.7 \ft \+it li. 1. c. 3. v. 10\+it*.\f*
\v 8 \f 1 \fr 8.8 \ft N\f* But \f b \fr 8.8 \ft This Philip a Phrygian was left in Ieruſalem by Antiochus to afflict the Iewes, \+it ch. 5. v. 22\+it*.\f* Philip ſeing the man to come forward by litle and litle, and that thinges for the more part ſucceeded with him proſperouſly, wrote to Ptolomee the gouernour of Cœleſyria and Phoenicia, to geue ayde to the kinges affaires.
\v 9 And he with ſpede ſent Nicanor the ſonne of Patroclus, of the principals of his freindes, geuing him of the nations mingled together, no leſſe then twentie thouſand armed men, to deſtroy vtterly al the ſtocke of the Iewes, adioyning alſo vnto him Gorgias a man of warre, and in martial affayres of very great experience.
\v 10 And Nicanor appointed, that he would ſupplie vnto the king the tribute that was to be geuen to the Romanes, two thouſand talents out of the captiuitie of the Iewes:
\v 11 and forthwith he ſent to the cities by the ſea ſide, calling men together to the buying of the Iewish ſlaues, promiſing that he would ſel ninetie ſlaues for a talent, not looking to the vengeance, which was to folow him from the Almightie.
\v 12 But Iudas when he vnderſtood it, shewed to thoſe Iewes that were with him, the coming of Nicanor.
\v 13 Of whom certaine fearing, and not crediting the iuſtice of God, fled away:
\v 14 and others if they had any thing left, ſold it, & withal beſought our Lord, that he would deliuer them from the impious Nicanor, who had ſold them before he came neere them:
\v 15 and if not for them, yet \f c \fr 8.15 \ft A iuſt and religious cauſe is the very beſt helpe, that can be in warres.\f* for the teſtament that was with their fathers, and for the inuocation of his holie & magnifical name vpon them.
\v 16 But Machabeus calling together ſeuen thouſand, that were with him, deſired that they would not be reconciled to the enemies, nor feare the multitude of the enemies coming againſt them vniuſtly, but would fight manfully:
\v 17 hauing before their eyes the contumelie, that was vniuſtly done by them to the holie place, and moreouer the iniurie alſo of the citie being made a laughing ſtocke, beſides alſo the ordinances of the anceſters broken.
\v 18 For they in dede truſt to their weapons, ſayd he, and to their boldnes alſo: but we truſt in the Almightie Lord, who can vtterly deſtroy both them coming againſt vs, and the whole world with one becke.
\v 19 And he admonished them alſo of the aydes of God, that were geuen to their fathers: and that vnder Sennacherib an hundred foure ſcore fiue thouſand perished. \x - \xt 4. Reg. 19.\x*
\v 20 And of the battel, that they had againſt the Galatians in Babylonia, how al they, when it came to the point, the Macedonians their felowes ſtaggering, being only ſix thouſand ſlewe an hundred twentie thouſand, by reaſon of the ayde geuen them from heauen, and for theſe thinges obteyned verie manie benefites. \f 1 \fr 8.20 \ft Of this battel with the Galatians there is no other mention in holy ſcripture but it ſemeth to be that, wherin they aſſiſted Antiochus the firſt called Soter, when he repelled the Galatians inuading Aſia: wherof Appianus writeth in \+it bellis Syriacis\+it*. And Ioſephus teſtifieth, \+it li. 12. c. 3\+it*. that Antiochus Magnus (ſonne of Soter) much fauored the Iewes, for their explottes donne in his fathers dayes.\f*
\v 21 With theſe wordes they were made conſtant, and readie to dye for the lawes, and their countrie.
\v 22 He appointed therfore his brethren captains ouer both orders, Simon, and Ioſeph, and Ionathas, vnder euerie one putting a thouſand and fiue hundreth.
\v 23 Beſide this alſo the holie booke being read vnto them by Eſdras,
\v 24 and a ſigne of Gods helpe being geuen, in the foreward the duke himſelf ioyned battel with Nicanor. And the Almightie being made their helper, they ſlew aboue nine thouſand men: and the greater part of Nicanors armie being made weake with woundes, they forced to flee.
\v 25 And taking away their money that came to buy them, they purſued them on euerie hand,
\v 26 but they returned being taken short with the time: for it was the day before the Sabbath: for the which cauſe they continewed not purſewing them.
\v 27 But gathering together the armour and ſpoiles of them, they kept the Sabbath: bleſſing our Lord that deliuered them this day, diſtilling the beginning of mercie vpon them.
\v 28 But after the ſabbath they diuided the ſpoyles to the feeble and to orphans, and to widowes: & the reſt themſelues had with their felowes.
\v 29 Theſe thinges therfore being done, and obſecration being made in common of al, they deſired our merciful Lord, that he would be reconciled to his ſeruants vnto the end.
\v 30 And of them that were with Timothee and Bacchides fighting againſt them, they ſlew aboue twentie thouſand, & wanne the high holdes: and they diuided manie prayes, making equal portion to the feeble, pupils, and widowes, yea and to the elder men.
\v 31 And when they had diligently gathered together their armour, they layd al together in conuenient places, and the reſidue of the ſpoiles they caried to Ieruſalem:
\v 32 and Philarches that was with Timothee, they ſlewe, a wicked man, which in manie thinges had afflicted the Iewes.
\v 33 And when they kept the feaſt of victorie in Ieruſalem, him that had burnt the holie gates, that is, Calliſthenes, when he was fled into a certaine houſe, they burnt, a worthie reward being repayed him for his impieties:
\v 34 But the moſt impious Nicanor, who had brought a thouſand merchants to the ſale of the Iewes,
\v 35 being humbled through the helpe of our Lord by them, whom he eſteemed no bodie, laying aſide his garment of glorie, fleeing by the midland, came alone to Antioch, hauing gotten great infelicitie by the deſtruction of his armie.
\v 36 And he that had promiſed that he would render tribute to the Romanes of the captiuitie of Ieruſalem, now profeſſed that the Iewes had God their protectour, & that for him they could not be wounded, becauſe they folowed the lawes appointed by him. \f 1 \fr 8.36 \ft \+it li. 1. c. 4. v. 28\+it*.\f*
\c 9
\cd Antiochus repulſed from Perſepolis, 3. and hearing that his armie is ouerthrowne in Iurie: 9. wormes iſſuing from his bodie, intolerably ſtincking: 11. acknowlegeth his wicked deſertes: 14. promiſeth amendment, 18. writeth to the Iewes, praying them to obey him, and his ſonne, 28. and dyeth miſerably.
\p
\v 1 AT \f 1 \fr 9.1 \ft R\f* that time Antiochus returned out of Perſis dishonourably.
\v 2 For he had entered into the citie which is called \f a \fr 9.2 \ft A chief citie of Perſis, called Elymais. \+it li. 1. c. 6. v. 1\+it*.\f* Perſipolis, and he attempted to ſpoile the temple, and to oppreſſe the citie: but the multitude running together to armes, they were put to flight: and ſo it chanced that Antiochus after his flight returned with dishonour.
\v 3 And when he was come about Ecbatana, he vnderſtood the thinges that were done to Nicanor and Timothee.
\v 4 And ſwelling in anger, he thought that he might wreake the iniurie of them, that had put him to flight, vpon the Iewes. And therfore he commanded his chariotte to be driuen, iorneing without intermiſſion, the heauenlie iudgement vrging him forward, becauſe he ſpoke ſo proudly, that he would come to Ieruſalem, & make it an heape of the ſepulchre of the Iewes.
\v 5 But he that ſeeth al thinges our Lord the God of Iſrael, ſtroke him with an vncurable and inuiſible plague. For as he ended this verie ſpeach, a cruel plague of the bowels tooke him, and bitter torments of the inner parts:
\v 6 and in dede very iuſtly, as who had tormented the bowels of others with manie and new torments, albeit he by no meanes ceaſed from his malice.
\v 7 And beſide this replenished with pride, breathing fire in his minde againſt the Iewes, and commanding the matter to be haſtened, it chanced him going with violence to fal from the chariot, and his limmes to be vexed with a greeuous bruiſing of the bodie.
\v 8 And he that ſeemed to himſelf to rule euen ouer the waues of the ſea, replenished with pride aboue the meaſure of man, and to weye the heights of mountaines in a balance, now being humbled to the ground was caried in a portatiue ſeate, teſtifying the manifeſt power of God in himſelf:
\v 9 ſo that out of the bodie of the impious man, wormes crawled abundantly, and his liue flesh fel of for paynes, with his ſmel alſo & ſtinke the armie was anoyed.
\v 10 And he that a litle before thought to touch the ſtarres of heauen, him no man could carie for the intolerable ſtinke.
\v 11 Hereby therfore he begane, being brought from his great pride, to come to the knowlege of himſelf, admonished by the plague of God, his paines increaſing euerie moment.
\v 12 And when neither himſelf now could abide his owne ſtinke, thus he ſayd: It is reaſon to be ſubiect to God, & a mortal man not to thinke of himſelf equally with God.
\v 13 And this wicked man prayed to our Lord, \f b \fr 9.13 \ft Antiochus was in dede really and ſeriouſly greued, and truly acknowledged that his affliction was for his ſinnes, \+it li. 1. c. 6. v. 11\+it*. but he was not truly penitent for the offence committed againſt God & his neighbour but only for his ovvne calamitie & miſerie: & therfore could not obteyne \+it mercie\+it* to remiſſion of his ſinnes, nor releaſe of the punishment. So alſo the damned in hel, know & confeſſe that they are punished for their ſinnes, but haue not true repentance, for their offence againſt God.\f* of whom he was not to obteyne mercie.
\v 14 And the citie to the which he came in haſt to haue brought it to the ground, & to haue made it a ſepulchre of bodies heaped together, now wisheth to make it free:
\v 15 And the Iewes whom he ſayd he would not vouchſafe worthie, no not of burial, but would geue them to birds & wilde beaſtes to be ſpoiled, & deſtroy them with the litle ones, now he promiſeth to make them equal with the Athenians.
\v 16 The holie temple alſo, which before he had ſpoiled, he would adorne with goodlie donaries, and would multiplie the holie veſſels, and of his reuenewes would allow the charges perteyning to the ſacrifices.
\v 17 Beſides theſe thinges, that he would be a Iew alſo, & would walke through euerie place of the earth, and would declare the power of God.
\v 18 But the paines ceaſing not (for the iuſt iudgement of God was come vpon him) deſpayring he wrote to the Iewes by the maner of a ſupplication an epiſtle conteyning theſe words:
\v 19 To \sc HIS VERY GOOD SVBIECTES\sc* the Iewes the king & prince Antiochus, much health, & welfare, and to be happie.
\v 20 If you and your children farewel, & if thinges be with you to your mind, we geue very great thankes.
\v 21 And I being in infirmitie, and mindeful of you benignely, being returned out of the places of Perſis, & taken with a grieuous infirmitie, haue thought it neceſſarie to haue a care for the common profite:
\v 22 not deſpayring of myſelf, but hauing great hope to eſcape the ſicknes.
\v 23 But conſidering that my father alſo, at what times he led his armie in the higher places, he shewed who should take the princedome after him:
\v 24 that if any mishappe should chance, or hard tydings be told, they that were in the countries, knowing to whom the whole gouernement was committed, might not be trubled.
\v 25 Beſides this, conſidering that al the poteſtates, and bordering neighboures wayte for times, and expect the euent, I haue appointed my ſonne Antiochus king, whom, I hauing recourſe oftentimes to the higher kingdoms did commend to manie of you: & I haue writen to him that which is ſet downe here beneath.
\v 26 I pray you therfore, and deſire you mindful of the benefits both publike and priuate, that euerie one keepe his fidelitie to me, and to my ſonne.
\v 27 For I truſt that he wil deale modeſtly & gently, and folowing my purpoſe, and that he wil be common vnto you.
\v 28 Therfore \f c \fr 9.28 \ft Of this tyrant S. Cyprian geueth this cenſure: \+it li. de exhort. Martyrij. King Antiochus an inueterate enimie\+it* to al the good, \+it Nay, in Antiochus Antichriſt is expreſſed\+it*.\f* the murderer, and blaſphemer being very ſore ſtrooken, and as himſelf had handled others, in a ſtrange countrie among the mountaynes, with a miſerable death departed this life.
\v 29 But Philip his foſter brother remoued his bodie: who fearing the ſonne of Antiochus, went to Ptolomee Philometor into Ægypt. \f 1 \fr 9.29 \ft \+it * li. 1. c. 6. v. 17.\+it*\f*
\c 10
\cd Iudas Machabeus clenſeth the temple, and inſtituteth the feaſt of dedication. 10. Young Antiochus Eupator reigning Ptolomeus of diſguſt killeth himſelf with poyſon. 14. Iudas reſiſteth great forces of Gorgias, taketh certaine holdes from the Idumeans, punisheth traytors, and killeth manie enimies. 24. In his battel againſt Timothee is miraculouſly aſſisted from heauen: 37. and finally killeth him.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 10.1 \ft P\f* Machabeus, and they that were with him, our Lord protecting them, \f a \fr 10.1 \ft This recouering and clenſing of the temple was after the fourth battel of Iudas, which was againſt Lyſias one of Antiochus chiefe captaines, as appeareth \+it li. 1. c. 4\+it*. and ſo was before the death of Antiochus, written here. \+it ch. 9\+it*.\f* recouered the temple & the citie againe:
\v 2 but the altars, which the aliens had ſet vp through the ſtreets, and alſo the temples he threw downe.
\v 3 And hauing purged the temple, they made an other altar: and out of fyred ſtones taking fire they offered ſacrifices after two yeares, and ſet incenſe, and lampes, and the breads of propoſition.
\v 4 Which thinges being done, they beſought our Lord proſtrate on the ground, that they might no more fal into ſuch euils: but and if they had ſometime ſinned, that they might be chaſtened of him more mildly, & not be deliuered to barbarous, & blaſphemous men.
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 10.5 \ft li. 1. c. 4. v. 5.\f* And what day the temple had bene polluted of the aliens, it happened that on the ſame day was made the purification, the fiue and twentith of the moneth, which was Caſleu.
\v 6 And with ioy eight dayes they kept in maner of tabernacles, remembring that a litle before they had kept the ſolemne day of Tabernacles in the mountaynes, and in dennes after the maner of beaſts.
\v 7 For the which cauſe they bare before them ſtalkes of herbs, and greene boughes, and palmes to him, that gaue ſucceſſe to clenſe his place.
\v 8 And they decreed by common precept, and decree to al the nation of the Iewes euerie yeare to keepe theſe dayes.
\v 9 And Antiochus that was called the Noble, his departure out of life was after this ſort. \f 1 \fr 10.9 \ft li. 1. c. 5. v. 1.\f*
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 10.10 \ft T\f* But now of Eupator the ſonne of Antiochus the impious, we wil tel the thinges that haue bene done, abbridging the euils that were done in the warres.
\v 11 For he hauing taken vpon him the kingdom, appointed ouer the affaires of the king one Lyſias general of the hoſt of Phænicia and Syria.
\v 12 For Ptolomee who was called Macer, determined to be a keeper of iuſtice toward the Iewes, and eſpecially for the iniquitie, that was done againſt them, and to deale peaceably with them.
\v 13 But being accuſed for this of his freinds to Eupator, when he was called oftentimes traytour, becauſe he had left Cypres commited vnto him by Philometor, and remouing to Antiochus the Noble, had reuolted alſo from him, he \f b \fr 10.13 \ft It is neuer an act of fortitude but of extreme paſillanimity when one in temporal miſerie killeth himſelfe to be ridde therof. But is a moſt heroical act to dye willingly for Gods glorie.\f* made an end of his life with poyſon.
\v 14 But \f c \fr 10.14 \ft Againſt this Gorgias Iudas had a victorie before in the time of Antiochus Epiphanes \+it li. 1. c. 4\+it*.\f* Gorgias being captayne of the places, taking vnto him ſtrangers often warred againſt the Iewes.
\v 15 And the Idumeans that kept the commodious holdes, receiued them that were chaſed from Ieruſalem, and attempted to make battel.
\v 16 And they that were with Machabeus, beſeeching our Lord by prayers that he would be their helper, made an aſſault vpon the holdes of the Idumeans:
\v 17 and ſticking to it with great force, they wanne the places, killed them that came in the way, & ſlewe altogether no leſſe then twentie thouſand.
\v 18 And wheras certaine were fled into two towres very ſtrong, hauing al prouiſion to make reſiſtance,
\v 19 Machabeus for the expugning of them, leauing Simon and Ioſeph, and alſo Zachæus, and ſuch as were with them very manie, himſelf turned to thoſe battels which forced more.
\v 20 But they that were with Simon, being led with couetouſnes, were perſwaded with money by certaine that were in the towres: and taking ſeuentie thouſand didrachmaes, they let certayne eſcape.
\v 21 But when it was told Machabæus what was done, aſſembling the princes of the people he accuſed them, that they had ſold their bretheren for money, their aduerſaries being let goe.
\v 22 Theſe therfore being become traytours he ſlewe, and forthwith he tooke the two towres.
\v 23 And with weapons and handes doing al thinges proſperouſly, in the two holdes he ſlewe more then twentie thouſand.
\v 24 And \f d \fr 10.24 \ft Timothee the ſecond captaine general of Antiochus with Bacchides was once before ouerthrowne. \+it li. 1. c. 8. v. 30\+it*.\f* Timothee, who before had bene ouercome of the Iewes, hauing called together an armie of foren multitude, and gathering horſemen of Aſia, came as to take Iurie by armes.
\v 25 But Machabeus and they that were with him, when he approched, beſought our Lord, ſprinkling their head with earth, and being girded about the loynes with heareclothes,
\v 26 lying flatte at the brimme of the altar, that he would be propicious to them, and an enemie to their enemies, and an aduerſarie to their aduerſaries, as the law ſaith.
\v 27 And ſo after prayer taking weapons, going forth ſomewhat far out of the citie, and being come very neere the enemies they pitched.
\v 28 And at the very firſt riſing of the ſunne both ioyned battel: theſe in deede hauing our Lord the ſuretie of victorie, and proſperitie with vertue: but they had courege for the captayne of the battel.
\v 29 But when there was a ſore fight, there appeared to the aduerſaries from heauen fiue men vpon horſes, comelie with golden bridles, conducting the Iewes:
\v 30 of whom two hauing Machabeus betwen them, compaſſing him round about with their armour, kept him ſafe: and againſt the aduerſaries they threw darts, & fire balles, wherby both confounded with blindnes, and filled with perturbation they fel.
\v 31 And there were ſlaine twentie thouſand fiue hundred, and horſemen ſix hundred.
\v 32 But Timothee fled into Gazara a ſtrong hold, wherof Chæreas was the captaine.
\v 33 And Machabeus, and they that were with him ioyfully beſieged the hold foure dayes.
\v 34 But they that were within, truſting to the place, blaſphemed aboue meaſure, & caſt forth abominable wordes.
\v 35 But when the fifth day appeared, \f e \fr 10.35 \ft Ioſephus Gorion, \+it li. 3. c. 13\+it*. ſaith theſe twentie zelous young men were of the \+it Aſsidians\+it*, who profeſſed a certaine religious forme of life: of whom mention is made before. \+it li. 1 ch. 2 v. 42. & c. 7. v. 13\+it*.\f* twentie yong men of them that were with Machabeus, incenſed in their mindes becauſe of the blaſphemie, went manfully to the wal, and with fierce conrege going on, they ſcaled to the top:
\v 36 Yea and the others alſo mounting vp, attempted to ſet the towres and the gates on fire, and to burne the blaſphemers themſelues aliue.
\v 37 And the hold being ſacked for two dayes together, they ſlewe Timothee that was found hyding himſelf in a certaine place: and his brother Chæreas, and Apollophanes they killed.
\v 38 Which thinges being done, they bleſſed our Lord in hymnes and confeſſions, who did great thinges in Iſrael, and gaue them the victorie.
\c 11
\cd Lyſias ſuppoſing with his armie of foureſcore thouſand footemen, & a great band of horſemen to ſubdue Ieruſalem: 6. Iudas with his few praying God, and going to fight, an Angel, in forme of an horſemen, goeth before them: 10. ſo they ſetting vpon the enemies kil manie, & the reſt flee. 13. Lyſias perceiuing Gods powre, offereth to procure peace. 22. Wherto the king, agreeth, writting to him, 27. and to the Iewes. 34. The Romanes alſo write to the Iewes.
\p
\v 1 BVT a litle after \f a \fr 11.1 \ft This Lyſias alſo had bene vanquiſhed before, \+it li. 1. c. 4. v. 28\+it*.\f* Lyſias the kings procuratour, and coſin, and chiefe ouer the affayers, being greatly offended with theſe thinges, that had hapened,
\v 2 hauing gathered foure ſcore thouſand, and al the horſemen, came againſt the Iewes, thincking that taking the citie, he should make it an habitation for the Gentiles:
\v 3 and he should haue the temple to make gayne of money, as the reſt of the temples of the Gentiles, and euerie yeare the prieſthood to be ſold:
\v 4 neuer recounting the powre of God, but furious in minde, he truſted in the multitude of foote men, and thouſandes of horſemen, and in foure ſcore elephants.
\v 5 And he entred into Iurie, and approching to Beth-ſura, which was in a narrow place from Ieruſalem the ſpace of fiue furlongs, he expugned that hold.
\v 6 But as Machabeus, and they that were with him, vnderſtood that the holdes were expugned, they beſought our Lord with weeping and teares, and al the multitude together, \f b \fr 11.6 \ft Knowing that the Patriarches Abraham, Iſaac, Iacob, likewiſe Moyſes Ioſue and manie others were ſingularly aſſiſted by Angelles, theſe Machabees in their good cauſe prayed for Angelical helpe, and had it: but ioyntly vvith their owne endeuour, although ſome times God geueth ſuch victories without cooperation of men. \+it Exo. 14. 4. Reg. 19\+it*.\f* that he would ſend a good Angel to the ſauing of Iſrael.
\v 7 And Machabeus him ſelf firſt taking weapons, exhorted the reſt together with him, to aduenture, and to geue ayde to their bretheren.
\v 8 And when they went forth together with prompt corege, at Ieruſalem there appeared going before them an horſeman in white clothing, with armour of gold, shaking a ſpeare.
\v 9 Then al they together bleſſed our merciful Lord, and tooke great corege: being readie to penetrate not only men, but alſo moſt fierce beaſtes, and walles of yron.
\v 10 They went therfore promptly, hauing an helper from heauen, and our Lord hauing pitie vpon them.
\v 11 And like lyons running violently vpon the enimies, they ouerthrew of them eleuen thouſand footmen, and of horſemen a thouſand ſix hundred:
\v 12 and they put to flight al, & very many of them being wounded ſcaped away naked. Yea and Lyſias him ſelfe shamefully fleeing eſcaped.
\v 13 And becauſe he was not ſenſeleſſe recounting with him ſelfe, the diminution made on his ſide, and vnderſtanding the Hebrewes to be inuincible, becauſe they reſted vpon the helpe of the almightie God, he ſent vnto them:
\v 14 and promiſed that he would conſent to al thinges, that are iuſt, and that he would force the king to be their freind.
\v 15 And Machabeus granted to Lyſias requeſtes, in al things hauing reſpect to the commonwealth and whatſoeuer Machabeus wrote to Lyſias, concerning the Iewes, the king granted it.
\v 16 For there were epiſtles written to the Iewes from Lyſias, conteyning this tenure: \sc LYSIAS\sc* to the people of the Iewes health.
\v 17 Iohn and Abſalom that were ſent from you, deliuering the wrytings, requeſted that I would accomplishe thoſe thinges which by them were ſignified.
\v 18 Therfore whatſoeuer might be brought to the king I declared vnto him: and that which the matters permitted I granted.
\v 19 If therfore you kepe fideletie in the affayres & henceforward, alſo wil endeuour to be a cauſe of doing you good.
\v 20 And concerning the reſt, word for word I haue geuen commandement both to theiſe, and to them that are ſent of me, to commune with you.
\v 21 Fare ye wel. In the yeare an hundred fourtie eight, of the moneth Dioſcorus the foure & twenteth day.
\v 22 But the kings epiſtle conteyned theſe thinges: King Antiochus to Lyſias \f c \fr 11.22 \ft Lyſias was in dede the kings coſin. \+it v. 35\+it*. but he calleth him brother for honour ſake.\f* his brother, health.
\v 23 Our father being tranſlated amongſt the goddes, we being willing that they that are in our kingdome should liue without truble, and employ diligence to their owne matters,
\v 24 we haue heard that the Iewes conſented not to my father to turne to the rite of the Greekes, but that they would keepe their owne inſtitution, and therfore that they requeſt vs their rites to be granted them.
\v 25 Being therfore deſirouſe that this nation alſo be quiet, oradyning we haue decreed, that the temple be reſtored vnto them, that they might doe according to the cuſtome of their anceſtours.
\v 26 Thou shalt do wel therfore if thou ſend to them, & geue the right hand, that our pleaſure being knowen they may be of good cheere, & looke to their owne commodities.
\v 27 But to the Iewes the kings epiſtle was in this manner: \sc KING\sc* Antiochus to the ſenate of the Iewes, and to the reſt of the Iewes health.
\v 28 If you fare wel, you are ſo as we would: yea our ſelues alſo fare wel.
\v 29 Menelaus came to vs, ſaying that you would come downe to your countrie men, that are with vs.
\v 30 To them therfore that come and goe, vntil the thirtith day of the month Xanthicus, we geue the right handes of ſecuritie,
\v 31 that the Iewes may vſe their owne meates, and their owne lawes, as alſo before: and that none of them by any meanes ſuffer moleſtation for theſe thinges, which haue bene done by ignorance.
\v 32 And we haue ſent alſo Menelaus to ſpeake to you.
\v 33 Fare ye wel. In the yeare an hundred fortie eight, of the moneth Xanthicus the fiftenth day.
\v 34 And the Romans alſo ſent an epiſtle, which is thus: QVINTVS Memmius, and Titus Manilius legats of the Romans, to the people of the Iewes health.
\v 35 Concerning theſe thinges which Lyſias the kings coſin hath granted you, we alſo haue granted.
\v 36 But touching the thinges which he thought good to be referred to the king, ſend ye forthwith ſome bodie, conferring diligently among your ſelues, that we may decree as is conuenient for you: for we goe to Antioch.
\v 37 And therfore make haſt to write agayne, that we alſo may know of what minde you are.
\v 38 Fate ye wel. In the yeare an hundred fourtie eight the fiftenth day of the moneth Xanthicus.
\c 12
\cd Whiles the Iewes haue peace with the king, others ſtil perſecute them. 5. which Iudas reuengeth. 13. and in Caſphin maketh great ſlaughter, and repoſeth in Characa. 19. Tenne thouſand of Timothees men are ſlayne. 20. whom Iudas purſuing killeth manie in Carnion: 34. taketh him, but releaſeth him againe: 27. the like in Ephron. 32. Some Iewes are ſlaine in battel againſt Gorgias. 38. Iudas and his men are purified, and gathering the dead bodies, finde that ſome had taken vnlawful ſpoiles. 42. For whoſe ſoules he prayeth, and cauſeth ſacrifice to be offered.
\p
\v 1 THESE couenants being made, Lyſias went foreward to the king, and the Iewes gaue themſelues to husbandrie.
\v 2 But they that ſtayed there, \f a \fr 12.2 \ft An other Timothee was ſlaine. \+it ch. 10. v. 37\+it*.\f* Timothie & \f b \fr 12.2 \ft Alſo an other Apollonius was ſlaine before. \+it li. 1. c. 3. v. 11\+it*.\f* Appollonius the ſonne of Gennaius, & alſo Ierom, & Demophon, beſides theſe alſo Nicanor the gouerner of Cyprus, did not ſuffer them to liue in reſt and quietnes.
\v 3 And the Ioppites committed a certaine flagicious fact, which was this: They deſired the Iewes with whom they dwelt, to goe into the botes, which they had prepared, with their wiues & children, as though no ſecret emnities were betwen them.
\v 4 Therfore according to the common decree of the citie, & they agreeing therto, & becauſe of the peace ſuſpecting nothing: when they were gone forward, into the depth, they drowned no leſſe then two hundred.
\v 5 Which crueltie Iudas as he vnderſtood to be done vpon the men of his nation, commanded the men that were with him: and inuocating God the iuſt iudge,
\v 6 he came againſt the murderers of his brethren, & the hauen he ſet on fire in the night, the botes he burnt, & them that were fled from the fire, he ſlew with the ſword.
\v 7 And when he had thus done theſe thinges, he departed as to returne againe, and to roote out al the Ioppites.
\v 8 But when he vnderſtood that they alſo, which were at Iamnia, would doe in like maner to the Iewes dwelling with them,
\v 9 he came vpon the Iamnites alſo by night, and ſet the hauen on fyre with the shippes, ſo that the light of the fire appeared at Ieruſalem \f c \fr 12.9 \ft A furlong is about the eight part of a myle, ſo this fire vvas ſene thirty miles of others count a furlong to conteyne a thouſand foote, the fifth part of a myle ſo it vvas ſene 48. myles diſtant.\f* two hundred fourtie furlongs of.
\v 10 When they were now departed thence nine furlongs, and made their iourney towards Timothee, the Arabians fiue thouſand men, and fiue hundred horſemen ioyned battel with them.
\v 11 And when there was a mightie battel, and by the helpe of God it had ſucceded proſperouſly, the reſt of the Arabians that were ouercome, beſought Iudas that the right hand might be geuen them, promiſing that they would geue paſtures, and profite them, in other thinges.
\v 12 And Iudas thinking in very deede that they might be profitable in manie thinges, promiſed peace, and right handes being taken, they departed to their tabernacles.
\v 13 And he ſet alſo vpon a certaine citie ſtrong with bridges, and enuironed with walles, which was inhabited with multitudes of heathen of al ſortes, the name wherof is Caſpin.
\v 14 But they that were within, truſting in the firmenes of the walles, & the prouiſion of victuals, dealt the more ſlackly, with reuiling wordes prouoking Iudas, and blaſpheming, and ſpeaking ſuch thinges as is not lawful to ſpeake.
\v 15 But Machabeus inuocating the great prince of the world, who without rammes and engines in Ieſus time threwe downe Ierico, fiercely aſſaulted the walles. \x - \xt Ioſue. 6.\x*
\v 16 And the citie being taken by the wil of our Lord he made innumerable ſlaughters, ſo that the poole adioyning of two furlongs in bredth, ſemed to runne died with bloud.
\v 17 From thence they departed ſeuen hundred fiftie furlongs, and they came to Characa to thoſe Iewes, that are called \f d \fr 12.17 \ft \+it Tubianei\+it*, or \+it Tubieni\+it*, ſignifie religiouſly good, it is probable that theſe vvere \+it the Aſſideans. li. 1. c. 2. v. 42. c. 7. v. 13\+it*.\f* Tubianeians:
\v 18 and in thoſe places they tooke not Timothee, and nothing being done he went backe, hauing left in a certaine place a very ſtrong garriſon.
\v 19 But Doſitheus, and Soſipater, who were captayns with Machabeus, ſlewe them that were left of Timothee in the hold, ten thouſand men.
\v 20 And Machabeus ordayning about him ſix thouſand, and placing them by bandes, went forth againſt Timothee, who had with him an hundred twenty thouſand footemen, & of horſemen two thouſand fiue hundred.
\v 21 And the coming of Iudas being knowen, Timothee ſent the wemen and children, and the other baggage before into the fortreſſe, that is called Carnion: for it was inuincible, and hard to come by, by reaſon of the ſtraites of the places.
\v 22 And when the firſt band of Iudas had appeared, feare was ſtroken into the enemies, by the preſence of God, who ſeeth al thinges, and they were put to flight one of an other, ſo that they were rather ouerthrowen of their owne companie, and were weakened with the ſtrokes of their owne ſwordes.
\v 23 But Iudas was exceding earneſt punishnig the prophane men, and he ouerthrewe of them thirtie thouſand men.
\v 24 And Timothee him ſelfe fel into the handes of Doſitheus and Siſipaters partes, and with manie prayers he beſought that he might be let go aliue, becauſe he had parents & brethren of manie of the Iewes, whom it might happen by his death to be deceiued.
\v 25 And when he had geuen his faith that he would reſtore them according to the appointmet they let him goe without harme, for the ſafetie of their brethren.
\v 26 And Iudas came backe from Carnion, hauing ſlayne twentie fiue thouſand.
\v 27 After the flight and ſlaughter of theſe, he moued his armie to Ephron a ſtrong citie, wherin a multitude dwelt of diuerſe nations: & ſtrong young men ſtanding before the walles reſiſted manfully, & in this were manie engins, and prouiſion of dartes.
\v 28 But when they had inuocated the Almightie, who with his power breaketh the forces of the enemies, they tooke the citie: and of them that were within they ouerthrew twentie fiue thouſand.
\v 29 From thence they departed to a citie of the Scythians, which was diſtant from Ieruſalem ſix hundred furlongs.
\v 30 But thoſe Iewes that were with the Scythopolitans teſtifying that they were vſed curteouſly of them, euen in the times of miſerie that they dealt modeſtly with them:
\v 31 geuing them thankes, and exhorting them alſo thence forward to be fauourable toward their ſtock, they came to Ieruſalem the ſolemne day of the weekes approching.
\v 32 And after Pentecoſt they went againſt \f e \fr 12.32 \ft Iudas had the victorie twiſe before againſt this Gorgias. \+it li 1. c. 4. v. 1. li. 2. c. 10. v. 14\+it*.\f* Gorgias the gouernour of Idumea.
\v 33 And he went forth with footemen three thouſand, and horſemen foure hundred.
\v 34 Who buckling together, it chanced few of the Iewes to be ſlayne.
\v 35 But Doſitheus one of the Bacenors an horſeman, a valiant man, held Gorgias: and wheras he would haue taken him aliue, a certayne horſeman of the Thracians came vpon him, and cut of his shoulder: and ſo Gorgias eſcaped into Mareſa.
\v 36 But they that were with Eſdrin, fighting long, and being wearied, Iudas inuocated our Lord to be their helper, and captayne of the battel:
\v 37 beginning in his countrey language, and with hymmes raiſing a crie, draue Gorgias ſouldiars into flight.
\v 38 And Iudas hauing gathered an armie came into the citie Odollam: & when the ſeuenth day came on, being purifyed according to the cuſtome, they kept the Sabbath in the ſame place.
\v 39 And the day folowing Iudas came with his companie, to take away the bodies of them that were ouerthrowen, and with their kinſmen to lay them in the ſepulchers of their fathers.
\v 40 And they found vnder the coates of the ſlayne ſome \f f \fr 12.40 \ft It was commanded. \+it Deut. 7. v 25\+it*. not to couet nor take anie thing perteyning to idols, but to deſtroy al. See this ſinne punished. \+it Ioſue. 7. 1. Reg. 15. &c\+it*.\f* of the donaryes of the idols, that were in Iamnia, from which the lawe forbiddeth the Iewes: therfore it was made playne to al, that for that cauſe they were ſlayne.
\v 41 Al therfore bleſſed the iuſt iudgement of our Lord, who had made manifeſt the hidden thinges.
\v 42 And ſo turning to prayers, they beſought him, that the ſame offence, which was committed, might be forgotten. But the moſt valient Iudas exhorted the people to kepe themſelues without ſinne, ſeing before their eyes what was done, becauſe of the ſinnes of them that were ouerthrowen.
\v 43 And making a gathering, he ſent twelue thouſand drachmes of ſiluer to Ieruſalem for ſacrifice to be offered for ſinne, wel and religiouſly thinking of the reſurrection.
\v 44 ( for vnles he hoped that they that were ſlaine, should riſe againe, it should ſeeme ſuperfluous, and vaine to pray for the dead)
\v 45 And becauſe he conſidered that they, which had taken their ſleepe \f g \fr 12.45 \ft Vnles it had bene the doctrine & practiſe of the Church to pray for the dead, Iudas could neuer haue thought of anie ſuch matter.\f* with godlines, had very good grace layd vp for them.
\v 46 It is therfore \f h \fr 12.46 \ft It is only profitable for thoſe that dye penitent.\f* a holie, and healthful cogitation to pray for the dead, that they may be looſed from ſinnes. \f 1 \fr 12.46 \ft li. 1. c. 6. v. 18.\f*\f 2 \fr 12.46 \ft This text is clere for praying for the dead.\f*
\c 13
\cd Menelaus a fugitiue Iewe is put to death. 9. Antiochus with his great armie is defeated twiſe, with loſſe of manie men. 23. Philippe rebelling, peace is renewed. 24. And Iudas is made Lord of Ptolemais.
\p
\v 1 IN the yeare an hundred fourtie nine Iudas vnderſtood, that Antiochus Eupator came with a multitude agaynſt Iurie,
\v 2 and with him Lyſias the procuratour, and cheefe ouer the affayres hauing with him of footemen \f a \fr 13.2 \ft In the firſt booke, \+it ch. 6 v. 30\+it* the number of this armie differeth from the number here recorded, the cauſe is for that ſome times thoſe only are counted vvhich vvere permanent, ſome times others are alſo counted vvhich came vncertainly. The like difficulties of differences occurre often in the bookes of kinges and Paralipomenon.\f* an hundred tenne thouſand, & of horſemen fiue thouſand, & elephants twentie two, chariots with hookes three hundred.
\v 3 And Menelaus alſo ioyned him ſelfe with them: and with much deceite beſought Antiochus, not for the weale of his contrie, but hoping that he should be appoynted to the principalitie.
\v 4 But the king of kinges ſtirred vp Antiochus mind againſt the ſinner, & Lyſias ſuggeſting that he was the cauſe of al the euils, he commanded (as the cuſtome is with them) that being apprehended he should be killed in the ſame place.
\v 5 And there was in the ſame place a tower of fiftie cubites, hauing an heape of ashes on euerie ſide: this had a proſpect ſteepe downe.
\v 6 From thence he commanded the ſacrilegious perſon to be throwne downe into the ashes, al thruſting him forward to death.
\v 7 And by ſuch law it chanced the tranſgreſſour of the law to dye: & Menelaus not to be put into the earth.
\v 8 And in deede very iuſtly, becauſe he committed manie offences toward the altar of God, the fyre and ashes wherof was holie: himſelf was condemned into the death of ashes.
\v 9 But the king furiouſe in mind, came to shew himſelfe worſe to the Iewes then his father.
\v 10 Which thinges when Iudas vnderſtood, he commanded the people that day and night they should inuocate our Lord, that as alwayes now alſo he would helpe them:
\v 11 as who were afrayed to be depriued of the law, & their countrie, & the holie temple: and that he would not ſuffer the people that had of late taken breath a litle while, to be againe ſubdued to the blaſphemous nations.
\v 12 Al therfore doing it together, and crauinig mercie of our Lord with weeping & faſtinges, being proſtrate for three dayes continually, Iudas exhorted them to prepare them ſelues.
\v 13 But he with the ancients conſulted, before the king should bring his armie to Iurie, and winne the citie, to goe out, and to committe the euent of the thing to the iudgement of our Lord.
\v 14 Committing therfore the power and charge of al to God, the creatour of the world, and hauing exhorted his companie to fight manfully, and to ſtand euen to death for the lawes, the temple, the citie, their countrie, and citizens: he placed his armie about Modin.
\v 15 And hauing geuen a ſigne to his companie of \f b \fr 13.15 \ft The vvatch word this night was: \+it The victorie of God\+it*.\f* the victorie of God, with moſt valient yong men piked out, by night ſetting vpon the kings court, in the campe he ſlew foure thouſand men, and the greateſt of the elephants, with them that had bene placed thereupon,
\v 16 and he filled the campe of the enemies with exceeding feare and perturbation, and the thinges being proſperouſly atcheiued, they departed.
\v 17 And this was done when the day appeared, the protection of our Lord helping him.
\v 18 But the king hauing taken a taſte of the hardines of the Iewes, attempted the difficultie of the places by policie:
\v 19 and he moued his campe to Bethſura, which was a ſtrong hold of the Iewes: but he was put to flight, he fel, he was diminished.
\v 20 And to them that were within Iudas ſent neceſſaries.
\v 21 But one Rhodocus of the Iewes armie vttered the ſecretes to the enemies, who being ſought for was apprehended, and shut vp.
\v 22 Againe the king had talke with them that were in Bethſura: he gaue the right hand: he tooke it: he went away.
\v 23 He ioyned battel with Iudas, he was ouercome. And as he vnderſtood that Phillippe rebelled at Antioch, who was left ouer the affayres, diſmayed in mynde intreating the Iewes, and yelding vnto them, he ſweareth concerning al thinges that ſeemed reaſon, & being reconciled he offered ſacrifice, honoured the temple, and gaue hoſts.
\v 24 He embraced Machabeus, & made him gouernor & prince from Ptolemais euen to the Gerrenians.
\v 25 But after, as he came to Ptolemais the Ptolemaians tooke greuouſly the couenant of amitie, being offended leſt perhaps they would breake their league.
\v 26 Then Lyſias went vp to the iudgement ſeate, and declared the reaſon, and appeaſed the people, and returned to Antioch: and in this maner the kinges iorney and returne proceded. \f 1 \fr 13.26 \ft li. 1. c. 7. v. 1.\f*
\c 14
\cd Demetrius hauing taken certaine holdes from Antiochus, 3. Alcimus lately depoſed from the office of high prieſt, 6. accuſeth good men of diſloyaltie, 11. others alſo incenſe Demetrius. 12. Wherupon Nicanor being ſent into Iurie, maketh Alcimus againe highprieſt: 18. hath peace with Iudas, conuerſing with him moſt familiarly. 26. But by Alcimus ſuggeſtion, ſeeketh to deliuer him into the kings handes: 30. otherwiſe threatneth to prophane the temple. 34. The prieſtes pray God to protect them. 37. Razias being accuſed, and purſued, 41. in extreme diſtreſſe killeth himſelf with meruelous corege of ſpirite.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 14.1 \ft Y\f* after the ſpace of three yeares Iudas vnderſtood, and they that were with him, that Demetrius the ſonne of Seleucus with a ſtrong multitude, and with shippes was come vp by the port of Tripolis to commodious places,
\v 2 and held the countries againſt Antiochus, and his gouernor Lyſias.
\v 3 And one \f a \fr 14.3 \ft Alcimus was of Aarons ſtoek. \+it li. 1. c 7. v. 14\+it*. but for this apoſtaſie here mentioned was vncapable of high prieſthood, & ſo Matthathias vvas ordayned being of the ſame progenie and moſt ſincere in religion.\f* Alcimus that had bene highprieſt, but voluntarily was contaminated in the time of the confuſion, conſidering that there was ſaftie for him by no means, nor acceſſe to the altar,
\v 4 came to king Demetrius in the yeare an hundred fiftie, offering him a crowne of gold, & a palme, & beſides theſe, \f 1 \fr 14.4 \ft \+it a golden bough\+it*.\f* Talloes, which ſeemed to be of the temple. And that ſame day in deede he held his peace.
\v 5 But hauing gotten a commodious time for his madnes, being called of Demetrius to counſel, and asked on what thing and counſels the Iewes reſted, he anſwered:
\v 6 They of the Iewes that are called \f b \fr 14.6 \ft This deſcription of the Aſſideans made by their malicious enimie in calumnious and odious termes, ſheweth vvel their ſingular zele, & ſinceritie in promoting Gods ſeruice. And ſo their aduerſaries malignant accuſations more againſt them then al others, is a plaine teſtimonie of their more rare and more ſingular vertues.\f* Aſſidians, of whom Iudas Machabeus is captaine, nourish battels, and moue ſeditions, neither doe they ſuffer the realme to be quiet.
\v 7 For I alſo being defrawded of my anceſtours glorie (I meane of the high prieſthood) am come hither:
\v 8 principally in deede keping fidelitie to the kings commodities, but ſecondly alſo prouiding for my citizens, forby their naughtines al our ſtocke is not a little vexed.
\v 9 But al theſe thinges being knowen, ô king, prouide I pray thee, both for the countrie, & for our ſtocke according to thy humanitie published to al men.
\v 10 For as long as Iudas is aliue, it is vnpoſſible that there be peace to the affayres.
\v 11 And ſuch thinges being ſayd of him, the other freinds alſo behauing themſelues as enemies againſt Iudas, incenſed Demetrius. \f 1 \fr 14.11 \ft li. 1. c. 7. v. 26.\f*
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 14.12 \ft A\f* Who forthwith ſent Nicanor, chiefe ouer the elephants captaine into Iurie:
\v 13 geuing him commiſſion, that he should take Iudas himſelfe: but them that were with him, he should diſperſe, & make Alcimus the high prieſt of the greateſt temple.
\v 14 Then the nations, which had fled from Iudas out of Iurie, ioyned themſelues by troupes with Nicanor, \f c \fr 14.14 \ft Apoſtates and politikes make their gayne by ſpoyling the faithful.\f* eſteming the miſeries, and calamities of the Iewes the proſperities of their owne affayres.
\v 15 The Iewes therfore hauing heard of Nicanors coming, & the aſſemblie of the nations, being ſprinkled with earth beſought him, that ordayned his people to kepe them for euer, and that protecteth his portion by euident ſignes.
\v 16 And the captaine commanding forthwith they remoued from thence, and they came together to the caſtel of Deſſau.
\v 17 And Simon the brother of Iudas had ioyned battel with Nicanor: but he was made afrayd with the ſodaine coming of the aduerſaries.
\v 18 Neuertheles Nicanor hearing the manlines of Iudas companions, and greatnes of courege, that they had for the conflicts of their countrie, was afrayde to make tryall by bloud.
\v 19 Wherfore he ſent Poſidonius, and Theodotius, & Mathias before to geue and take the right handes.
\v 20 And when there was long conſultation of theſe thinges, and the captaine himſelf had moued it to the multitude, there was one ſentence of them al to accord vnto amitie.
\v 21 Therfore they appointed a day, wherin they might ſecretly deale among themſelues, and ſeates were brought forth and ſette for euery one.
\v 22 But Iudas commanded armed men to be in places conuenient, leſt perhaps ſome miſchefe might ſodainly ariſe from the enemies: & they made agreable communication.
\v 23 Nicanor abode at Ieruſalem, and did nothing vniuſtly, and the flockes of the multitudes, that had bene gathered together he diminished.
\v 24 And he eſteemed Iudas alwayes deare from the hart, and he was inclined to the man.
\v 25 And he deſired him to marrie a wife, and to beget children. He made a marriage, he liued quietly, and they liued in common.
\v 26 But Alcimus ſeeing their charitie one towardes an other, and the couenantes, came to Demetrius, and ſayd that Nicanor aſſented to forraine matters, and that he ment to make Iudas being a traytour to the kingdom, his ſucceſſour.
\v 27 Therfore the king being exaſperated with this mans moſt wicked criminations, wrote to Nicanor, ſaying, that he in deede was greatly diſpleaſed for the couenant of their amitie: neuertheles that he commanded him to ſend Machabeus quickly priſoner to Antioch.
\v 28 Which thinges being knowen Nicanor was amaſed, and tooke it greuouſly, if he should vndoe thoſe thinges which they had couenanted, being nothing hurt of the man.
\v 29 But \f d \fr 14.29 \ft Nicanor a right worldlie politike, a figure of Pilate and of ſuch temporizing Iudges, counſellers, and courtiers, as lacke zele in religion.\f* becauſe he could not reſiſt the king, he obſerued oportunitie wherby to accomplish the commandement.
\v 30 But Machabeus ſeeing that Nicanor dealt with him more auſterely, and that he exhibited his accuſtomed meeting more ſternely, vnderſtanding this auſteritie not to be of good, a few of his companie gathered together, he hid himſelf from Nicanor.
\v 31 Which when he vnderſtood that he was ſtoutly preuented of the man, he came to the moſt great & moſt holie temple: and the prieſtes offering the accuſtomed hoſtes, he commanded the man to be deliuered vnto him.
\v 32 Who \f e \fr 14.32 \ft They knew not preciſely vvhere Iudas vvas, neither vvould they ſearch for him to deliuer him to the perſecutor.\f* ſaying with an oath, that they knew not where he was, that was demanded, ſtretching out his hand to the temple,
\v 33 he ſware, ſaying: Vnles you wil deliuer Iudas priſoner vnto me, I wil beate downe this temple of God to the flat ground, and wil digge downe the altar, and this temple I wil conſecrate to \f f \fr 14.33 \ft Bacchus called alſo \+it Liber\+it*, and by manie other names, feaned by infidels to be auctor and god of wine. And therfore drunkards dedicate feaſtes & temples to him.\f* Liber pater.
\v 34 And when he had ſayd theſe thinges, he departed. But the prieſtes ſtretching forth their hands vnto heauen, inuocated him that was alwayes the defender of their nation, ſaying thus:
\v 35 Thou ô Lord of al, which lackeſt nothing, wouldeſt a temple of thy habitation to be made amongſt vs.
\v 36 And now ô Lord holie of al holies, preſerue for euer this houſe impolluted, which of late hath bene clenſed.
\v 37 And Razias one of the ancients of Ieruſalem, was accuſed to Nicanor, a man that was a louer of the citie, and wel reported of, who for his affection was called father of the Iewes.
\v 38 This man long time kept the purpoſe of continencie in Iudaiſme, and content to geue his bodie and life for perſeuerance.
\v 39 But Nicanor willing to manifeſt the hatred that he had againſt the Iewes, ſent fiue hundred ſouldiars to take him.
\v 40 for he thought if he had intrapped him, that he should doe the Iewes verie great hurt.
\v 41 But the multitudes coueting to rush into his houſe, and to breake open the gate, & to ſet fyre therto, when he was in taking, \f g \fr 14.41 \ft S. Auguſtin epiſt. \+it 61. ad Dulcitium & l. 2. c. 23. ad epiſt. 2. Gaud\+it*. diſcuſſing this fact; ſaith the holie ſcripture doth tel it, not praiſe it. As to be admired not to be imitated, & that either it vvas not vvel done by him, or at leaſt is not conuenient in this time of grace.\f* he ſtrooke himſelfe with a ſword:
\v 42 chooſing to dye nobly rather then to be made ſubiect to ſinners, and againſt his noble birth to ſuffer vnworthie iniuries.
\v 43 But wheras for haſt he had not made the wound with a ſure ſtroke, and the multitudes brake in within the dores, running backe boldly to the wal, he threwe downe him ſelfe manfully headlong vnto the multitudes:
\v 44 who quickly geuing place to his fal he fel vpon his necke.
\v 45 And when he had breathed, incenſed in minde he aroſe: and when his blood ranne with a great ſtreame, & he was wounded with moſt greuous wounds, running he paſſed through the multitude:
\v 46 and ſtanding vpon a certaine ſteepe rocke, & now being become without bloud, gryping his bowels, with both handes he caſt them vpon multitudes, inuocating the dominatour of life and ſpirit, that he would reſtore theſe to him againe: & ſo he departed this life. \f 1 \fr 14.46 \ft li. 1. c. 7. v. 39.\f*
\c 15
\cd Nicanor intending to aſſault Iudas on the ſabbath day, 5. blaſphemeth moſt proudly. 7. Iudas with al confidence in God encorregeth his men. 11. confirming them with the relation of a viſion in ſlepe. 21. So he with feruent prayer, the enemie truſting his owne ſtreingth, ioyneth battel. 27. and killeth thirtie fiue thouſand, and Nicanor amongſt the reſt. Whoſe head, and hand cut of with the shoulder are hanged vp in Ieruſalem: 33. his tongue geuen to the birdes, and a feſtiual day obſerued. 38. And herewith the Auctor (of this ſecond booke) concludeth the whole hiſtorie.
\p
\v 1 BVT Nicanor as he vnderſtood that Iudas was in the places of Samaria, he purpoſed with al violence to ioyne batel \f a \fr 15.1 \ft Prophane men make their aduantage of religious mens good conſcience, but Iudas rightly inſtructed in this caſe defended his iuſt cauſe alſo in the ſabbath. \+it li. 1. c. 3. v. 40\+it*.\f* the day of the Sabbath.
\v 2 But the Iewes that of neceſſitie folowed him, ſaying do not ſo fearcely and barbarouſely, but geue honour to the day of the ſanctification, and honour him that beholdeth al thinges:
\v 3 that vnhappie man asked, if there were a powre in heauen, that commanded the ſabbath day to be kept.
\v 4 And they anſwering: There is the liuing Lord himſelf in heauen, the potent, that commanded the ſeuenth day to be kept.
\v 5 But he ſayd: And \f b \fr 15.5 \ft O Luciferian blaſphemie!\f* I am potent vpon the earth, that commanded armes to be taken, and the kings affayres to be accomplished. Neuertheles he obteyned not to accomplish his counſel.
\v 6 And Nicanor in dede puffed vp with exceeding pride, had thought to haue ſet vp a common victorious memorie of Iudas.
\v 7 But Machabeus alwayes truſted with al hope that there would come ayde from God to them.
\v 8 And exhorted his companie, that they should not feare at the coming of the nations, but should haue in minde the aydes geuen vnto them from heauen, and now should hope that they should haue the victorie from the Almightie.
\v 9 And ſpeaking vnto them out of the law, and the Prophetes, admonishing them alſo of the conflictes, that they had made before, he made them the more prompt:
\v 10 and ſo their hartes being encoreged, withal he shewed the fraude of the Gentils, and their breaking of oathes.
\v 11 And he armed euerie one of them, not with ſenſe of buckler and ſpeare, but with very good words, & exhortations, \f c \fr 15.11 \ft This dreame was from God as the effect shewed. And Iudas knew alſo that it ſo was by internal inſpiration, as Ioſeph \+it Mat. 1. v. 20\+it*. was aſſured of his dreames.\f* declaring a dreame worthy to be credited, wherby he reioyced them al.
\v 12 And the viſion was in this maner: Onias who had bene the highprieſt, a good and benigne man, reuerent to behold, modeſt of maners, and comelie of ſpeach, and who from a childe was exerciſed in vertues, that he ſtretching forth the handes prayed for al the people of the Iewes.
\v 13 After this that there appeared alſo an other man meruelous for age, and glorie, and for the port of great dignitie about him.
\v 14 And that Onias anſwering ſayd: This is a louer of his bretheren, & of the people of Iſrael: this is he that prayeth much for the people, & for the whole citie, Ieremie the Prophete of God.
\v 15 And that Ieremie put forth his righthand, and \f d \fr 15.15 \ft Ieremie \+it (ch. 38. v. 17\+it*.) perſuaded the king of Iuda to yeld himſelf with the citie and people to the Chaldees, and not to reſiſt. But now he deliuereth a ſword to Iudas, exhorting him to fight: according to Gods wil in diuers caſes, and times, for \+it there is a time of warre, and a time of peace. Eccle. 3. v. 8\+it*.\f* gaue vnto Iudas a ſword of gold, ſaying:
\v 16 Take the holie ſword a gift from God, wherwith thou shalt ouerthrow the aduerſaries of my people Iſrael.
\v 17 Being exhorted therefore with the wordes of Iudas exceeding good, by which the coreges might be ſtured vp, and the hartes of the youngmen ſtreingthned, they reſolued to fight, and to encounter manfully: that manhood might decide the matter, becauſe the holie citie, and the temple were in danger.
\v 18 \f e \fr 15.18 \ft Gods honour, & holie thinges are firſt and principally to be reſpected, before wordlie freindes, though they alſo muſt be regarded in due order and place.\f* For there was leſſe care for their wiues, and children, and alſo for their bretheren, and kinſemen: but the greateſt and principal feare was for the holines of the temple.
\v 19 And they alſo that were in the citie, tooke no litle care for them that were to ioyne battel.
\v 20 And when they did al hope that iudgement would be geuen, and the enimies were preſent, and the armie was ſet in aray, the beaſtes & horſemen diſpoſed in conuenient place,
\v 21 Machabeus conſidering the coming of the multitude, and the varietie of the prouiſion of armour, and the fiercenes of the beaſtes, ſtretching forth his handes vnto heauen, he inuocated our Lord, that worketh wonders, who not according to the might of armes, but according as it pleaſeth him, geueth victorie to the worthie.
\v 22 And he ſayd inuocating in this maner: \x - \xt 4. Reg. 19.\x* Thou Lord which didſt ſend thyne Angel in the time of Ezechias king of Iuda, and didſt kil an hundred eightie fiue thouſand of the campe of Sennacharib:
\v 23 & now ô Dominatour of the heauens ſend thy good Angel before vs, in feare and trembling of the greatnes of thyne arme,
\v 24 that they may be afrayde, which with blaſphemie come againſt thyne holie people. And he in dede ended his prayer thus.
\v 25 But Nicanor, and they that were with him, with trumpets & ſongues came nere.
\v 26 But Iudas, and they that were with him, inuocating God by prayers ioyned battel:
\v 27 with the hand in dede fighting, but in their hartes praying to our Lord, they ouerthrew no leſſe then fiue and thirtie thouſand, being greatly delighted with the preſence of God.
\v 28 And when they had ceaſed, and returned with ioy, they vnderſtood that Nicanor was ſlaine, for al his armour.
\v 29 A shout therfore being made, and a great crie, they bleſſed the Almightie Lord in their countrie language.
\v 30 And Iudas, who by al meanes was in bodie and mind readie to dye for his citizens, commanded that Nicanors head, and hand with the shoulder being cut of, should be caried to Ieruſalem.
\v 31 Whither when he was come, hauing called his countrimen, and the Prieſtes to the altar, he ſent alſo for them that were in the caſtel,
\v 32 And shewing them the head of Nicanor, & the wicked hand, which he ſtretching forth againſt the holie houſe of almightie God, had mightely bragged.
\v 33 The tongue alſo of impious Nicanor being cut out, he commanded to be geuen pecemeale to the birdes: and the hand of the furious man to be hanged vp againſt the temple.
\v 34 Al therfore bleſſed the Lord of heauen, ſaying: Bleſſed be he, that hath kept his place vndefiled.
\v 35 And he hung vp Nicanors head in the toppe of the caſtel, that it might be an euident, & manifeſt ſigne of the helpe of God.
\v 36 Therfore al by common counſel decreed, by no meanes to let paſſe this day without ſolemnitie:
\v 37 but to kepe the ſolemnitie the thirtenth day of the moneth Adar, which is called in the Syrian language, the day before Mardocheus day. \f 1 \fr 15.37 \ft li. 1. c. 8. v. 1. & c.\f*
\v 38 Theſe thinges therefore being done concerning Nicanor, & \f f \fr 15.38 \ft More being written in the firſt booke, this auctor maketh one concluſion of al, becauſe other perſecuters being alſo ouercome the land was againe caulme after ſtormes.\f* from that time the citie being poſſeſſed of the Hebrewes, I alſo in theſe wil make an end of ſpeaking.
\v 39 And if wel, and as is competent for a ſtorie that myſelf alſo would: but if not ſo worthely it reſteth to be pardoned me.
\v 40 For as to drinke alwayes wine, or alwayes water is hurtful, but to vſe now one now an other is delectable: ſo to the readers, if the ſpeach be alwayes exact, it wil not be gratful. Here therefore it shal be ended.
\s1 THE CONTINUANCE OF THE CHURCH AND RELIGION IN THE SIXTH AGE: FROM THE CAPTIVITY in Babylon to the coming of our Saviour, near the space of 640. years.
\ip SUCH is the providence of Almighty God, that not obscurely, or at some times only, but manifestly, and without intermission his Divine Majesty is acknowledged, his name glorified; his \f - \ft God's true Service hath always continued in the visible Church.\f* Religion professed, and his precepts observed by a visible known Church, from the beginning of the world to the end thereof, as we have already shewed in the other five ages; and shall no less clearly declare the same in this sixth. For albeit the peculiar people of God were for their sins carried forth of their country, and held captives in Babylon seventy years, and after their reduction were subject to strangers ruling over them, and sometimes extremely afflicted with persecution, yet they still persevered in the same faith and religion, had succession of Priests, and of one High priest, with conservation also of the royal line of David, even to Christ our eternal King and Priest.
\ip First therefore concerning Articles of faith and religion, the belief in one God was so generally \f - \ft Faith in one God is the ground of all Religion.\f* confessed by the whole Jewish nation, that their Priests and Prophets did use it for a principle, in confirmation of other points, as well doctrinal as moral. So Malachie teaching that our neighbour is to be beloved, God to be served, and his laws to be kept: \it Is there not one Father of us all\it* (saith he. ch. 2. v. 10.) \it Hath not one God created us? Why then doth every one of us despise his brother, violating the covenant of our fathers?\it* More expressly Jeremy in his Epistle (Barac. 6.) sheweth the vanity and absurdity of many gods, exhorting the people to serve the one omnipotent God, saying to him sincerely in their hearts (v. 5.) \it Thou oughtest to be adored, o Lord\it*. Likewise, when the Magicians of Chaldea ascribed the knowledge of dreams to false gods, Daniel with the other three children (ch. 2. v. 18.) \it prayed the God of heaven: and the mystery was revealed to Daniel\it*. And he declared and expounded the King's dream. Who thereupon confessed to Daniel (v. 47.) \it In very deed your God is the God of gods, and Lord of kings\it*. The same three children (Daniel 3.) were cast into the burning furnace, and Daniel into the lions' den (ch. 6. & 14.) ready to die for their faith in one God. For this faith also Mardocheus, as is written in the book of Esther, was persecuted, and he with all the people were in extreme danger. And the author of the book of wisdom teacheth that one God is known by consideration of his creatures: \it All men are vain\it* (saith he. ch. 13. v. 1) \it that by things seen, understand not him that is: neither attending to the works agnise who was the workman\it*. So the author of Ecclesiasticus ch. 1. v. 8. professeth: \it There is one most high Creator omnipotent, and mighty King, and to be feared exceedingly, sitting upon the throne, the God of Dominion\it*.
\ip As for the high Mystery of \it three Divine Persons in one God\it* not so commonly revealed in the \f - \ft The B. Trinity revealed to some and uttered obscurely in the old testament.\f* old testament, yet was it known and in some sort uttered: As Aggeus 2. v. 5. & 6. \it I am with you, saith the Lord of hosts, the word that I did covenant with you when you came out of the land of Aegypt: and my Spirit shall be in the midst of you\it*. Where, by \it the Lord of hosts\it*, is commonly understood \it God the Father; by his spirit, God the Holy Ghost\it*, and the \it word\it* may signify \it God the Son\it*: of whose Incarnation the Prophet plainly speaketh in the next verses. For in this consisteth the covenant between God and his people, that they should keep \it his word\it* of precepts and commandments expressed in the law: and he would send them \it the word, his only Son the Second Divine Person\it* to redeem mankind. Again the same three Persons seem to be distinguished in diverse places. \it God the Father\it* is described according to man's small capacity, Daniel 7. v. \f - \ft Distinction of Persons in one God.\f* \f - \ft The Father.\f*
\ip 8. thus: \it Thrones were set, and THE ANCIENT OF DAYS sat: his vesture white as snow, and the hairs of his head as clean wool, his throne flames of fire, his wheels fire kindled\it*. He is called \it Ancient of days\it*, not only because he is eternal, for so are the other two Divine Persons, but this term is attributed to the Father, because in order he is the beginning, from whom the other two Persons proceed (The Son by generation, the Holy Ghost from the Father and the Son by procession.) To \it God the son\it* \f - \ft The Son.\f* the same Prophet Daniel prayeth: ch. 9. v. 17. saying: \it Now therefore hear o our God, the petition of thy servant, and his prayers: and shew thy face upon thy Sanctuary which is desert, for thine own sake\it*: that is, for thine own merits: which can only be understood of that Divine Person, which is incarnate. Zachary 12. v. 10. God speaking by the Prophet saith: \it I will pour out upon the house of David, and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the spirit of grace and of prayers\it*, which may easily be understood to be the promise of the B. Trinity: but that which immediately followeth: \it & they shall look towards me, whom they pierced\it*, can only be spoken by the Second Divine Person, who only is incarnate, and was pierced in his Passion. In the book of wisdom is much written of wisdom increated, \it a term appropriated to\it* God the Son \it . especially ch. 2. 7. 8. 9.\it* and \it 10.\it* The like in Ecclesiasticus, ch. 1. 4. 24. And ch. 51. v. 14. is distinct mention of the Father & the son: \it I have invocated\it* (saith the author, or any faithful soul) \it our Lord, the Father of my Lord\it*. There is \f - \ft The Holy Ghost.\f* likewise particular mention of the Holy Ghost in some places. As 2. Esd. 9. v. 20. \it Thou gavest them the good Spirit, which should teach them\it*. for the office of internal teaching is appropriated to the \it Holy Ghost\it*. Joan 14. v. 17. and 16. v. 13. \it The spirit of truth, & he shall teach you all truth\it*. Ezec. 36. v. 27. \it I will put my Spirit in the midst of you, and will make that you walk in my precepts\it*. Zach. 7. v. 12. \it The words which the Lord sent in HIS SPIRIT, by the hand of the former Prophets\it*. Sap. 1. v. 5. \it The Holy Ghost of discipline will fly from him that feigneth\it*. Ecclesiasticus 1. v. 9. \it He created her in the Holy Ghost\it*. 24. v. 29. \it They that eat ME, shall yet hunger, and they that drink ME, shall yet thirst\it*.
\ip Where God calleth \it the Holy Ghost\it* (which is received by grace) himself. Because all three Divine Persons are one God. And that there be many Divine Persons in God who is one in substance, \f - \ft Other places prove plurality of Persons in God.\f* is sufficiently signified by all those holy Scriptures, where God is called by the name \it Elohim\it*, in the plural number; especially seeing this name hath also the singular number, \it Eloha\it*. As Job. 12. v. 4. & 36. v. 2. Daniel 2. v. 28. Habacuc. 1. v. 11. & 3. v. 3. Which last place seemeth most plainly to speak of \it the Son of God, ELOHA MITHEMAN IAVO. God will come from Theman\it*, or from the South. And therefore where this word \it Elohim\it* is used in the plural number (as in most places it is) it signifieth plurality of Persons in God.
\ip Christ's Incarnation is more clearly foreshewed by the Prophets, who above other \f - \ft The mystery of Christ's Incarnation is more frequent & more plain in the holy Scriptures especially in the Prophets.\f* consolations, most especially comforted the people by their prophecies of Christ our Saviour.
\ip Jeremie 23. v. 5. \it I will raise up to David a just branch, and he shall reign a King, and shall be wise, and he shall do judgement and justice in the earth\it*. Ch. 31. v. 23. \it A woman shall compass a man\it*.
\ip Christ though in body a little infant, yet in power and wisdom was most perfect of all men, even when he was in his mother's womb. Ch. 33. v. 14. \it Behold the days will come, saith our Lord, and I will raise up the good word\it*. v. 15. \it I will make the spring of justice to bud forth unto David, & he shall do judgement and justice in the earth\it*. Jeremy's Lamentations are in greatest part of Christ and \f - \ft Jeremy.\f* his Church. And some part can hardly be applied to any other. ch. 3. v. 30. \it He shall give the cheek to him that striketh him, he shall be filled with reproaches\it*. c. 4. v. 20. \it Christ our Lord is taken in our sins\it*. Baruc. 2. v. 35. God promising to reduce the people from Babylon, addeth: \it And I will establish\it* \f - \ft Baruch.\f* \it unto them another testament everlasting\it* (by Christ, whose Kingdom is for ever) \it that I be their God, and they shall be my people\it*. Ch. 3. v. 36. \it This is our God, and there shall none other be esteemed against him\it*. v. 38. \it After these things he was seen upon the earth, and was conversant with men.\it* \f - \ft Ezech.\f*
\ip \it Ezechiel\it* peculiarly \it called\it* by an Angel \it the son of man\it*, was therein a special figure of our Saviour, who so calleth himself. And the same Prophet hath in plain terms foreshewed the office of Christ the true Pastor of all pastors. ch. 24. v. 25. \it I will\it* (saith God by his Prophet \it raise\it* ) \it up over them one Pastor, who shall feed them, my servant David\it*; that is, Christ prefigured by David. His admirable visions in the three first chapters, and nine last pertain properly and principally to the new Testament of Christ, and his Church, shewing the aboundance of grace and glory given by him to the elect. Daniel 7. v. 13. \it With the clouds of heaven there came in as it were the Son of man, and\it* \f - \ft Dan.\f* \it he came even to the ancient of days, and in his sight they offered him\it*. He came even to the ancient of days, because in his Divinity he is equal to the Father: and in his humanity he is offered to God in Sacrifice. v. 14. \it His power is eternal: and his Kingdom shall not be corrupted\it*. ch. 9. v. 24. \it Seventy weeks\it* (of years) \it are abridged\it*, that sins may be forgiven, grace be infused, prophecies be fulfilled, and the Holy one of holies be anointed. All which belong only to Christ. v. 26. \it After sixty two weeks Christ shall be slain\it*. Aggeus 2. v. 8. \it The desired of all nations shall come\it*. Zach. 3. v. 8. \it I will\it* \f - \ft Aggeus.\f* \f - \ft Zacharias.\f* \it bring my servant the Orient\it*. ch. 13. v. 7. \it Strike the Pastor, and the sheep shall be dispersed\it*, fulfilled in Christ's Passion Mat. 26. v. 21. Malach. 3. v. 2. \it Forthwith shall come to his temple the Dominator,\it* \f - \ft Malachias.\f* \it whom you seek, & the Angel of the testament, whom ye desire\it*. The book of wisdom. ch. 2. v. 12. \f - \ft Also the books of wisdom.\f* describeth the malice of the wicked against Christ: \it Let us\it* (say they) \it circumvent the just, because he is unprofitable to us: and he is contrary to our works, and reproachfully objecteth to us the sins of the law\it*. v. 13. \it He boasteth that he hath the knowledge of God, and nameth himself the Son of God\it*. \f - \ft Ecclesiasticus.\f* Ecclesiasticus 24. v. 34. \it God appointed to David his servant, to raise up a King of him, most strong and sitting in the throne of honour for ever\it*. Which eternal King proceeding from David can be none but Christ our Saviour. And all the praises of Patriarchs, & Prophets (in the last eight chapters) consist in their faith, and expectation of Christ. Likewise the Priests and people 1. Mach. 14. \f - \ft & Machabees.\f* v. 28. 35. and 49. shewed their faith of Christ to come, when they established Simon and his progeny in the government and high priesthood, for ever, \it till there rise the faithful Prophet\it*, to wit the Prophet of whom all the Prophets did speak (Luc. 24. v. 27.)
\ip Amongst the rest Jeremy, ch. 31. v. 23. and Ezechiel ch. 44. v. 2. make also especial mention of \f - \ft Prophecies, & figures of the B. Virgin Mother of God.\f* some singular privileges of the most excellent Virgin Mother of God. Of whom also Judith and Esther were apparent figures, who received special graces for the benefit of their nation, and so did this singular Virgin receive of God most eminent gifts, above all other mere creatures, for the benefit of the whole Church.
\ip Of Angels the celestial spirits, is frequent mention in the holy Scriptures of this age. Their \f - \ft Angels excel corporal creatures in multitude.\f* multitude is innumerable, and therefore are insinuated to men by general terms. Daniel. 7. v. 10. \it Thousands of thousands ministered to him, and ten thousand hundred thousands assisted him\it*. \f - \ft And in power.\f* And their power is most great, and to men most profitable. An Angel defended the three children in the furnace, walking with them in the fire. Daniel 3. v. 49. 95. Another defended Daniel \f - \ft They help men and are lawfully invocated by men.\f* from the lions. ch. 6. v. 22. The same or another carried Habacuc from Jury into Babylon. Dan. 14. v. 35. And restored him in his place again. v. 38. The Archangel Gabriel instructed Daniel, ch. 8. v. 16. 17. ch. 9. v. 21. And ch. 10. v. 13. & 20. Other Angels the Patrons or Guardians of the Persians and Grecians, prayed for those countries; and S. Michael v. 21. for the Jews. An Angel spake in Zachary. ch. 1. v. 9. Another Angel went to meet him. ch. 2. v. 3. And in respect of Angelical offices, both S. John Baptist, and our Saviour himself are figuratively called Angels. Malach. 3. v. 1. No marvel therefore that Judas Machabeus and his army, 2. Machab. 11. v. 6. \it prayed for the assistance of a good Angel\it*, which was granted them. v. 8. And so \it they went promptly, having an helper from heaven\it*. v. 10. Their like prayer had the same effect in another battle. 2. Machab. 15. v. 27.
\ip Contrary to these glorious Angels are \it other spirits\it*, at first created in grace, which falling into \f - \ft Devils hating God, and all mankind never cease to tempt men to sin.\f* pride and most obstinate malice, are perpetual enemies to God their Creator, and to all mankind, continually calumniating the works of God, and of all his servants, whereof they are \it called Devils\it*, or \it calumniators\it*. They never cease tempting all they can to evil, so to bring men to eternal death:
\ip For \it by the envy of the devil\it* (Sap. 2. v. 7.) \it death\it* (both of soul & body) \it came into this world\it*. The just stipend of sin. All sins offend God and please the devils. But more particularly they desire to \f - \ft They seek to be honoured with sacrifice.\f* be honoured as God with \it Sacrifice\it*. Which therefore they require to themselves and their \it idols\it*. And for this sin of Idolatry, above all others, God is most provoked to wrath: & for the same most especially punished his people: as the Prophet Baruch (ch. 4. v. 6.) signifieth to the people, saying: \it You are sold to the Gentiles\it*, &c. \it You are delivered to their adversaries\it*: and giving the reason why, he addeth v. 7. \it For you have exasperated him that made you, the eternal God, immolating to devils and not to God\it*.
\ip The same all the Prophets teach, and withal that \it Sacrifice is the sovereign service due to God\it* \f - \ft Sacrifice is the proper service of God.\f* \it only\it*, and not to any creature, how excellent soever. But of Sacrifice there is so much written, that it were over long and needless to recite the places. It importeth more to observe the predictions of the most excellent, and perfect Sacrifice of the new Testament. Malachie 1. v. 11. \it From the\it* \f - \ft Sacrifice of the new testament, in all places.\f* \it rising of the sun\it* (saith God by this Prophet) \it even to the going down, there is sacrificing, and there is offered in my name a clean oblation\it*. In the old testament they offered cattle, & birds, by pouring out their blood about the altar, and drawing forth their bowels. For purging and cleansing whereof there was much washing and labour: but now in the Church of Christ, is \it the clean Sacrifice\it* of \f - \ft More pure and excellent than the old.\f* our Lord's body and blood, in forms of bread and wine. It is also in it-self so pure, that it \it cannot be polluted\it* (as the old sacrifices were, v. 12.) by unworthy Priests, but is always available to some or other, ex opere operato. According to that the same Prophet testifieth, ch. 3. v. 4. \it The Sacrifice of Juda and Jerusalem shall please our Lord\it*. Which is necessarily understood of the Christians' sacrifice: for else this place were contrary to that which God said to the Jewish Priests. ch. 1. v. 10. \it I have no will in you, and I will not receive gift at your hand\it*. Daniel also prophesieth, ch. 9. v. 27. \f - \ft The old ceased and the new succeeded.\f*
\ip that \it in the half of the week the host and the sacrifice shall fail\it*. Ch. 12. v. 11. \it The continual sacrifice shall be taken away\it*, thereby signifying that not only after the figure, the Sacrifice prefigured should succeed (for else there should be no daily Sacrifice at all in the new Testament, which Malachie saith plainly there shall be, not in one, or in few places, \it but from the rising of the sun, even to the going down\it*, &c.) but also that both the old and new sacrifices should be taken away in their several times. For so our Saviour (Mat. 24. v. 15.) applieth the next words of this prophecy, \it and\it* \f - \ft Which shall also be abandoned by Antichrist.\f* \it abomination of desolation shall be set up\it*, not only as a sign before the destruction of Jerusalem, but also of the end of the world. Verified in part as in the figure, when the temple was destroyed, & diverse profanations made in the same place: but more especially shall be fulfilled by Antichrist, abolishing the holy Sacrifice of Christ's body and blood, so much as he shall be suffered: as S.
\ip Hyppolitus writeth, lib. de Antichristo, & in oratione de consummatione mundi. Agreeable to S.
\ip Ireneus. lib. 4. c. 32. & li. 5. in fine S. Jerome. in Dan. 12. Theodoretus in eundem locum, and S.
\ip Chrysostom in opere imperfecto. Yea some \it Hebrew Rabbins acknowledge Transubstantiation\it*, in \f - \ft Transubstantiation confessed by Rabbins.\f* the Eucharist, as R. David Kimbi witnesseth upon these words of Osee. 14. v. 8. \it They shall live with wheat, & shall spring as a vine\it*: Many of our Doctors (saith he) expound this, that there shall be mutation of nature in wheat, in the times of our Redeemer Christ.
\ip This Rabbi David also, and the Chaldee Paraphrasis expound Ezechiel's Prophecy, ch. 36. v. 25. \it I will pour out upon you clean water\it*, of the remission of sin, though they signify not by what particular means. Which Christian Doctors undoubtedly explicate of \it the Sacrament of Baptism\it*. \f - \ft Baptism.\f*
\ip And likewise his other prophecy, ch. 47. v. 1. \it waters issued forth under the threshold of the house towards the East\it*, cannot be understood of any other waters than of Baptism.
\ip The \it purifications, oblations\it*, and other works of penance practised by the people, after their return from captivity, written. 2. Esd. 9. 10. 13. testify their observation of the law in this point, by which \it the Sacrament of penance\it* in the new testament was prefigured. \f - \ft Penance.\f*
\ip In like sort the \it continuance of Priesthood\it*, and Priestly functions is manifest in the books of Esdras, \f - \ft Holy Orders.\f* and of other Prophets, which prefigured \it the Sacrament of holy Orders\it*, in the Church of Christ.
\ip In these times also the \it feasts\it* instituted by the law, were observed with more or less solemnity, as \f - \ft Feasts.\f* time, place, and other opportunities served. As Esdras testifieth. li. 1. c. 3. v. 2. \it Iosue\it* (the high-Priest) \it & Zorobabel\it* (the duke) after their return from captivity, \it built an altar\it* (notwithstanding the threats of infidels) \it & offered upon it holocaust to our Lord morning & evening. And they made the solemnity of tabernacles, and other feasts, as well in the Calends, as in all the solemnities of our Lord\it*, though the temple was not yet built again (v. 6.) And afterwards upon new occasion, \it Judas Machabeus\it*, 1. Mach. 4. & 2. Mach. 10. instituted a new feast, which our Saviour observed. Ioan. 10. v. 12.
\ip The like observation was kept of \it fasts\it*. For amongst the feasts which were all duly performed (1. Esd. 3. v. 5.) one was of \it Expiation\it*, which consisted in \it fasting from even to even\it*. Levit. 23. Num. 29. And besides the ordinary, Esdras appointed a peculiar fast for special purposes, 1. Esd. 8. \f - \ft Feasts ordinary.\f* \f - \ft And extraordinary.\f* v. 21. \it And I proclaimed\it* (saith he) \it a fast, beside the river Ahava, that we might be afflicted before the Lord our God: and might desire of him a right way for us and our children\it*. And (v. 23.) \it we fasted and besought our God hereby: and it fell out prosperously unto us\it*. Again, 2. Esd. 9. v. 1. \it The children of Israel came together in fasting, and sackcloths, and earth upon them\it*. See more of fasting Judith. 4. & 9. Esther. 5. & 14. Zachar. 8. And of Abstinence from certain meats according to the law, \f - \ft Abstinence.\f* Daniel 1. & 9. Judith. 10. & 12. 2. Mach. 9. & 7.
\ip More generally \it the whole form of good life\it* is excellently prescribed in the books of wisdom and \f - \ft Form of good life prescribed in the sapiential books.\f* Ecclesiasticus. Where under the general virtues of \it Wisdom\it* and \it Justice\it*, all are admonished to seek diligently to know God, and to serve him. As much as to say, to have faith and good works: the two feet, and legs, on which the godly walk unto life everlasting. Let one short sentence here serve for example (wishing all men to read more in the books themselves) Sap. 6. v. 18. 19. & 20. is this gradation. \it The beginning of wisdom is the true desire of discipline; the care of discipline is love; & love is the keeping of her laws: & the keeping of the laws is the consummation of incorruption: & incorruption maketh to be next to God\it*. These are the steps from earth to heaven, from this vale \f - \ft The ascending by steps from earth to heaven.\f* of miseries to eternal happiness. First, \it a true\it* and sincere \it desire of discipline\it*, or of God's true service: 2. This desire or \it care of discipline\it* breedeth \it love\it* of God: 3. \it love is the keeping of laws\it*, the commandments of God: for he that saith he loveth God, and keepeth not his commandments is \f - \ft 1. Ioan. 2.\f* a liar: 4. \it Keeping the laws is the consummation of incorruption\it*: making the soul perfect in virtues, & free from corruption of sins: 5. \it and\it* this \it incorruption maketh to be next to God\it*, joining man with God, which is the perfect beatitude of eternal life. And so he concludeth. v. 22. \it Therefore\it* (from first to last by degrees) \it desire of wisdom leadeth to the everlasting kingdom\it*. Yet must we understand \f - \ft Without God's grace preventing, no man can think or do any thing meritorious.\f* that neither the first step of good desire, nor any of the rest is in a man's own power as of himself, so much as to think a good thought, but God's grace preventeth, stirreth men up, & \f - \ft \+it 2. Cor. 3.\+it*\f* continually assisteth, in all good beginnings, progress, & perseverance, as the same divine author teacheth a little before, v. 14. \it wisdom preventeth them that covet her, that she first may shew herself unto them\it*. Then to admit or refuse is in their power, that have good motions. And therefore sin is rightly imputed, and damnation justly inflicted upon the wicked: because as Nehemias (2. Es. 9. v. 17.) testifieth of the ungrateful people, \it they would not hear. And they hardened their necks, and gave the head to return to their servitude as it were by contention\it*, or striving against God, through their own free will; which appeareth here to remain in sinners. On the other side the same \f - \ft Confidence of good works done in grace.\f* Nehemias in confidence of reward for good works, and of his voluntary cooperating with God's grace, feared not to pray (2. Esd. 5. v. 19.) in these words: \it Remember me my God to good, according to all things which I have done to this people\it*.
\ip Some men moreover besides the commandments of the law, voluntarily professed a peculiar \f - \ft Voluntary vows like to Evangelical counsels.\f* state of holy life, a plain figure, or rather an example of Evangelical counsels. As in the former ages the Nazarites, whose rule is prescribed Numeri 6. practised by Sampson (Iudic. 13.) & Samuel (1.
\ip Reg. 1.) & the Rechabites (Jer. 35.) so in this last age next before Christ \it the Assideans\it*, or \it Esseni\it*. 1.
\ip Mach. 2. v. 42. of whom Judas Machabeus in his time was head or captain. 2. Mach. 14. v. 6. \f - \ft Perpetual virginity.\f*
\ip Jeremie the prophet (ch. 16. v. 2.) by God's ordinance lived single unmarried all the time of the captivity. \it Thou shalt not take a wife, and thou shalt not have sons and daughters in this place\it*, to wit in Jerusalem. Neither did he marry when he was afterward in Aegypt. But of his own accord remained a virgin all his life, as S. Jerome writeth, li. 1. advers. Iovinianum.
\ip Prayer of Saints after they are departed from this world is manifestly deduced of the sacred \f - \ft Prayers of Saints.\f* text, Jere. 15. v. 1. of Moyses and Samuel, not to be heard if they should pray for the people, whom God had decreed to punish, were consequently to be heard in some other case. And more expressly. 2. Mach. 15. v. 12. & 14. is recorded that \it Onias and Jeremy did pray for all the people, & for all the holy city\it*. Reverent \it estimation of Relics\it* and other holy things is manifest by the fact \f - \ft Relics.\f* of the same Prophet Jeremie, who by God's ordinance (2. Mach. 2. v. 1. & 5.) \it hid the holy fire, and the Tabernacle, and the Ark, & the altar of incense in a cave\it*, that they should not be profaned by infidels ransacking Jerusalem, and the temple: Other \it holy\it* ornaments also, and \it vessels\it* were \f - \ft Holy vessels.\f* restored by the favourable King Cyrus, 1. Esd. 1. v. 7. & ch. 8. v. 30. In figure also of \it the holy\it* \f - \ft Sign of the Cross.\f* \it Cross\it* on which Christ was to redeem mankind, \it those that mourned for the abominations in Jerusalem\it* (Ezec. 9.) \it were signed in their foreheads with\it* the letter \it Thau\it*, or T. and so were saved from the common slaughter of the unsigned.
\ip \it Prayer and Sacrifice for the dead\it* is likewise clear 2. Mach. 12. v. 43. &c. if either the text may be \f - \ft Prayer for the dead.\f* admitted for Canonical, saying (v. 46.) \it It is a holy & healthful cogitation to pray for the dead\it*; or for good testimony of Judas' fact, being High-priest, and doing that which the whole Church practised, and which the Jews yet observe to this day.
\ip Of the \it General Resurrection\it*, is good testimony in the same place v. 43. and 44. as the ground of \f - \ft Resurrection.\f* Judas his piety towards the dead, \it Well and religiously thinking of the Resurrection. For unless he hoped that they which were slain should rise again, it should seem superfluous and vain to pray for the dead\it*. But seeing he did believe the Resurrection, he did right well and piously. And seeing the belief of resurrection is true, it followeth, as this author inferreth, that it is a holy thing to pray for the dead.
\ip Malachie the last of the Prophets, in the last chapter foresheweth, and describeth the \it General\it* \f - \ft Judgement.\f* \it judgement\it*, in the end of this world: wherein the wicked shall be condemned, and the just eternally rewarded. Which \it day shall come\it* (saith he) \it kindled as a furnace. All that do impiety\it* (dying in \f - \ft Everlasting punishment, and eternal reward.\f* that state) \it shall be stubble, and that day shall inflame them. And there shall rise to you that fear my name, the Sun of justice, and health in his wings\it*, or glorious beams, healing and curing all bodily infirmities & defects. Before which day he foretelleth of two signs, v. 5. \it The coming of Elias\it* the \f - \ft The coming of Elias, & conversion of the Jews.\f* Prophet, and v. 6 & the \it conversion of the Jews to Christ\it*. And thus much may here suffice, for particular points of religion in this age.
\ip It resteth to view the state and government of the Church in this time which may be considered \f - \ft The state of the Church in the times of heathen Monarchies in general.\f* according to the four Monarchies of heathen nations, the Chaldees, the Medes & Persians, the Grecians, and the Romans. Under the Chaldees, whose Emperial city was Babylon, they were in captivity seventy years. By the Medes and Persians (for that Monarchy consisted of those two nations) they were released from captivity with many favours, yet sometimes afflicted. Under the Monarchy of the Grecians, they were partly in extreme \it persecution of Antiochus Epiphanes\it*, & of other Grecian Kings & Princes, partly in wars for defence of God's laws. Before and after which persecution and wars, as well under the Grecians as the Romans till Christ's Passion, the Church was for most part in peace, yet sometimes afflicted. But omitting many intricate difficulties about the times and reigns of sundry heathen Kings, it will suffice our purpose, to shew the general state of the Jewish nation, with their own particular governers spiritual and temporal, with more or less favour of foreign Princes.
\ip First therefore concerning their estate in their captivity in Babylon, we may here observe \f - \ft Their state in the captivity.\f* God's providence, in that before the city and temple of Jerusalem were destroyed, and the whole nation made captive, \it Ioachin\it* (otherwise called Jechonias) the son of Joachaz (who was also called Jechonias) \it King of Juda was transported into Babylon, and his mother\it*, and many other principal persons. (4. Reg. 24. 15.) Likewise \it Josedech son of Saraias high Priest\it* (1. Paral. 6. v. 15.) was carried into Babylon. And in the mean time Sedecias (uncle to Ioachin) reigned in Juda, who in the eleventh year was taken and carried captive into Babylon, and there died, Ioachin yet living in prison. And Saraias the High-priest with others was slain in Rebla, when Jerusalem was destroyed. 4. Reg. 25. v. 18. & 21. To whom Josedech succeeded in the high-Priesthood. So that both the issue of David, in the right line of our Saviour's genealogy, and the High-Priest of Aaron's stock, \f - \ft King Jechonias, and Josedech High-Priest were in Babylon before the whole nation was captive.\f* were in Babylon before the whole body of the nation was brought thither. This \it Jechonias\it* (or Ioachin) remained in prison, till the death of Nabuchodonosor, the space of thirty seven years, and was then delivered by Evilmerodach, and by him entertained courteously as a Prince, 4. Reg. \f - \ft Jechonias entertained in captivity as a Prince.\f* 25. v. 27. He married there and had issue \it Salathiel\it*; and Salathiel had \it Zorobabel\it*. Who together with \it Josue\it* son of Josedech high-Priest, & \it Esdras, Nehemias\it*, & others recited 1. Esd. 2. \it conducted the children of Israel from Babylon into their country\it*. There were also in a former transmigration \f - \ft Daniel with other three children were carried before any of the Kings into Babylon.\f* \it Daniel\it* and the other three children, \it Ananias, Misael\it*, & \it Azarias\it*, (of the royal or principal blood) \it in the third year of Ioakim\it* (otherwise called Eliacim son of Iosias. 4. Reg. 23. v. 34.) \it King of Juda\it*. Dan. 1. v. 1. 6. These with others were carried as hostages into Babylon, and brought up more liberally. Where serving God sincerely, & abstaining from unlawful meats, they were protected by God, much also esteemed & promoted in that place. For Daniel about the age of twelve years, convinced the two wicked Judges, and delivered Susanna from their cruel hands. Dan. 13. And afterwards for declaring and interpreting the King's dream (Dan. 2.) and excellent wisdom, and gift of prophecy, was admired by all, advanced by the King: but maligned by certain envious sorcerers and great \f - \ft They were singularly esteemed.\f* men. Whereby he was sometimes in great danger, but still delivered by God's power \f - \ft Sometimes in danger.\f* protecting him. Dan. 6. & 14. The other \it three children\it* were likewise \it advanced\it* Dan. 2. v. 49. and \f - \ft But preserved by God.\f* therefore by diverse envied, & \it for refusing to adore an idol\it* set up by Nabuchodonosor were \it cast into\it* a hot burning \it furnace\it*, and there preserved. Dan. 3.
\ip Jeremie, who before this time began to prophesy whiles he was a child, (Jere. 1.) continued in the \f - \ft Jeremy prophesied in Jerusalem and in Aegypt.\f* time of captivity, in Jerusalem and Jury, with much affliction, and still prophesying finally died in Aegypt. \it Baruch\it* his scribe, and also a Prophet, went sometimes into Babylon, and returned into Jury (Baruch. 1.) instructing and exhorting the people.
\ip \it Ezechiel\it* was carried with King Jechonias and Josedech into Babylon, and there prophesied (ch. 1. \f - \ft Ezechiel and Daniel in Babylon.\f* v. 2.) part of the same time with Daniel, in great part the same things with Jeremie. And during the captivity, King Jechonias, Josedech the high-Priest, Jeremie, Baruch, Ezechiel Prophets, & innumerable others (some Martyrs, and many Confessors) parted from this world. But Daniel yet lived. And in place of Josedech Highpriest Josue succeeded, and the progeny of King Jechonias continuing in Salathiel and Zorobabel, the nation had them and other eminent men, with temporal dependence upon foreign Princes in the next Monarchy of the Medes and Persians.
\ip For when \it Darius\it* King of Medes had slain Baltazar King of the Chaldees, and so possessed \f - \ft The Monarchy of Medes and Persians.\f* Babylon, with the whole country, he \it brought the Monarchy to the Medes and Persians\it*. Dan. 5. v. 31. and within the space of one year he died; and Cyrus succeeding granted leave to all the Jews \f - \ft Cyrus licensed the Jews to return and build up their temple.\f* to return into Jury; and there to build up their temple, and city of Jerusalem, which Nabuchodonosor had destroyed. At which time Daniel had his vision, that \it Christ\it* our Saviour should come into the world, \it within seventy weeks\it* (of seven years to the week, that is, in four hundred ninety years) after the perfect finishing of the temple and city. Dan. 9. v. 24. & 25. But \f - \ft Prophecy of Christ, after seventy weeks.\f* \it when they were so built again\it*, that the weeks began to be counted, is very obscure: as it was the will of God, that the prophecy, being certain in it-self, should not be over clear to every man's understanding, but as likewise many other \it prophecies, shut and sealed\it*. Dan. 12. v. 6. 9. 13.
\ip In this time of the Medes and Persians' Monarchy, \it Mardocheus\it* remaining in Chaldea, after the \f - \ft Mardocheus.\f* relaxation \it had that vision\it* in a dream, Esther. 11. after which followed the history of him, & \f - \ft Esther.\f* \it Queen Esther\it*, and wicked \it Aman\it*; with the danger and delivery of all the Jews in those parts. \f - \ft Aman.\f*
\ip Some think it likewise probable that the history of Judith happened after the captivity; though \f - \ft Judith.\f* others suppose that it was in the time of Manasses King of Juda: which not being our purpose to discuss and decide, we will pass to things more certain.
\ip The Prophets \it Aggeus\it* & \it Zacharias\it* near twenty years after the relaxation, earnestly exhorted \f - \ft Aggeus and Zacharias.\f* the Princes & people to build up the temple, which had been begun, and now was neglected upon vain fear, thinking \it the time was not yet come of building the house of our Lord\it*. Aggeus 1. v. 2.
\ip whereupon \it the Prophet reproveth them\it*, expostulating thus: \it Why, is time for you to dwell in embowed houses, and this house\it* (of our Lord) \it desert?\it* And assureth them v. 10. \it that their ground\it* should remain barren, & ch. 2. v. 15. their \it sacrifice ungrateful, till they should build the temple\it*:
\ip promising moreover that this new temple should be more glorious by Christ's personal presence therein, than the former temple, built by Salomon. But specially the Church of Christ \f - \ft The Church more glorious in the new testament.\f* prefigured of by the temple, should far excel the Synagogue of the old testament. ch. 2. v. 10. \it Great shall be the glory of this last house more than of the first\it*. Which Zacharie confirmeth inviting the Gentiles to come and the Jews to return into Christ's Church: ch. 2. v. 6. \it O fly out of the land of the North, saith our Lord, because into the four winds of heaven have I dispersed you\it*. v. 7. \it O Sion fly thou that dwellest with the daughter of Babylon\it*. And by diverse other visions & prophecies they foreshew the conversion of the Gentiles, & rejection of the Jews for their obduration but in the end they also shall be converted.
\ip Malachie prophesied after the finishing of the temple, exhorting all to offer their sacrifices with \f - \ft Malachie.\f* purity of heart, reprehending both priests and people for not so doing. ch. 1. He also foresheweth the rejection of the Jews, & calling of the Gentiles, with the change of the old sacrifices, and institution of a new far more excellent, and more effectual, to be offered \it every-where\it* (v. 10. & 11.) He concludeth his prophecy ch. 4. foretelling the terrible day of Judgement, and \it life or death everlasting\it*.
\ip These later prophets yet living, as Josephus, Eusebius, Theodoretus and others testify in their histories, the Grecians obtained so great a Monarchy by king Alexander the Great of Macedo, \f - \ft The Grecian Monarchy.\f* that being parted after his death amongst many, yet all were great kingdoms, some longer some shorter time. In the beginning whereof when king Alexander came to Jerusalem, as Josephus \f - \ft King Alexander honoured Iaddus the High-Priest.\f* writeth li. 11. c. 8. Antiquit. \it Iaddus\it* the \it High Priest\it* going forth in his pontifical attire to meet him, the same King straightways fell down at his feet with all reverence. And being demanded by his friends, the Princes of his army, why he so much honoured the High-Priest, he answered, that he honoured not the man for himself, but for his office, and God in him, who had appeared to him in sleep in that very habit, and ornaments, when he in Macedonia discoursed in his mind of making battle against the Persians, promising him assured victory. Shortly after this it happened, that Manasses an Apostata High Priest, by intercession of Sanaballat, whose daughter he had unlawfully married, obtained licence to build a temple in Garizim, which the Samaritanes \f - \ft The schismatical temple in Garizim.\f* afterwards pretended to be more ancient than the temple of Jerusalem, against which our Saviour gave sentence. Ioan 4. v. 22. It was also decided by King \it Ptolomeus\it* in Alexandria (as Iosephus witnesseth li. 13. c. 6.) by way of arbitrement, finding that the temple of Jerusalem, and the High-Priests thereof had a perpetual succession from Salomon's time, and that their pretence of Jacob's adoring in Garizim was not to the purpose, seeing there was no succession, that temple being lately built. Nevertheless the same Ptolomeus to gratify Onias and other Apostata, son of good Onias High-Priest and Martyr (2. Macab. 4. v. 34.) gave leave to build another temple in Aegypt, which stood likewise in schism against the true temple of Jerusalem, wresting to their \f - \ft Another schismatical Temple in Aegypt.\f* purpose the Prophecy of Isaie. ch. 19. v. 19. \it In that day there shall be an altar of our Lord in the midst of Aegypt\it*. Which S. Jerome sheweth to be understood of the Church of Christ. Before this last schismatical temple, and after the former were the Seventy two Interpreters, or Translators of \f - \ft The Seventy two Interpreters.\f* the Hebrew Bible into Greek. Of whom S. Jerome and all ancient Fathers speak much, and esteem of very great and Canonical authority.
\ip In the time of the Grecians' Monarchy, profane learning flourished more than before, and \f - \ft Profane learning flourished amongst the Grecians, but they erred exceedingly in matters of Religion.\f* Philosophers abounded, but differed exceedingly amongst themselves, & all erred in the principles both of \it Natural\it* & \it Moral\it* knowledge. For whereas indeed God omnipotent was the only maker of the whole world, and all things therein, all these Philosophers supposed & taught, that some material thing was coeternal with God: and so they put the same thing to have been the beginning of all other things. Which some say was the water, some the Air, some the Earth, some the \f - \ft Primum principium.\f* fire, some all these four Elements, some the Atoms, or indivisible small bodies, some one thing some another. Whereof S. Epiphanius writeth in compendio contra haereses. And the like absurd conceits they had of the chief Good, or \it Summum bonum\it*. Which \it the Pythagorians thought\it* \f - \ft Summum bonum.\f* \f - \ft Pythagoreans.\f* to be nothing else but a certain immortality of \it the soul\it*, and so, as it may still be in a body. And therefore seeing both men and beasts do die, they held opinion, that when a soul \it parteth out of one body\it*, it goeth \it into another\it*. Yea and maketh \it transmigration from one species\it* or kind \it to another\it*. As from a man's body into the body of a horse, or an oxe; and contrarywise from a brute beast into a man again, and from one beast into another. \it The Stoics put the chief good in virtues\it*, but could \f - \ft Stoics.\f* reach no further than to a certain contentment of joy in their mind, not knowing the reward of virtues to consist in seeing God. \it Platonics or Academics\it* conceived more of God, and pure \f - \ft Achademics.\f* spirits, but thought both corporal and spiritual creatures were coeternal with God. The \f - \ft Peripatetics.\f* \it Peripatetics\it* placed the \it chief good or felicity\it* in the \it aggregation\it* of best \it spiritual, corporal, and worldly things\it* together. The Epicures esteemed carnal and bodily pleasures above all. And all these \f - \ft Epicures.\f* and their followers judged so diversely of the right true felicity, contradicting and condemning each other's opinions, that they were multiplied into innumerable Sects. As S. Augustin declareth out of Marcus Varro: and opposeth against them all, the one assured faith and judgement of \f - \ft The assured faith of the Church, the city of God.\f* God's Church, in his 19. book de civit. Dei. c. 4. And concludeth with the Royal Prophet, and S.
\ip Paul, that their \it cogitations are vain, which will have happiness to be in any other thing but in seeing God\it*; or to be obtained by any other means without God's grace. And not only before & since, but also in the same times the \it authors of\it* The Books of \it wisdom\it* & \it Ecclesiasticus\it* taught right doctrine against those erroneous Philosophers.
\ip For profession also of true faith and religion the Machabees both suffered and laboured most \f - \ft The Machabees professed the same.\f* notably, when \it King Antiochus\it* Epiphanes (1. Mach. 1. v. 43.) \it wrote to all his kingdom, that all\it* \f - \ft Antiochus his Edict.\f* \it the people should be one, and every one should leave his own law. And whosoever should not do according to the word of Antiochus they should die\it*. Against which most wicked decree, and cruel execution thereof, God's grace so abounded that (v. 56.) \it many of the people of Israel, determined with themselves, that they would not eat the unclean things: and they chose rather to die, than to be defiled with unclean meats: and that they would not break the holy law of God, & so were murdered\it*:
\ip As is more particularly recorded, 2. Mach. 5. v. 14. how \it there were in the space of three days\it* \f - \ft Martyrs for this faith.\f* \it fourscore thousand slain, forty thousand imprisoned, & no less sold\it*. After this with more pretence of justice, but with more malice, endeavouring to terrify others, & to draw them to yield, or make shew of conformity to wicked laws, ch. 6. v. 10. \it two women were accused to have circumcised\it* \f - \ft For circumcision.\f* \it their sons whom when they had led about through the city, with the infants hanging at their breasts, they threw down headlong by the walls\it*. And v. 11. \it Other people were burnt with fire, for secretly\it* \f - \ft For keeping the Sabbath.\f* \it keeping the day of the Sabbath\it*. Thirdly v. 18. \it Eleazarus\it* being urged to eat swine's flesh, and entreated by his familiar friends to make shew of conformity, would neither eat, nor feign to eat it, but \it died most constantly, leaving an example of virtue & fortitude\it*. Fourthly \it seven brethren and\it* \f - \ft For abstaining from swine's flesh.\f* \it their mother\it* (2. Mach. 7.) yielded also their lives in most glorious Martyrdom, because they would not yield conformity to wicked laws.
\ip After which heroical constancy in suffering, it was also God's providence, that others should \f - \ft Holy wars for the Church and religion.\f* shew their fortitude, in delivering his Church from these calamities and dangers. For \f - \ft Matthathias.\f* \it Matthathias\it* of the tribe of Levi, and stock of Aaron Priest, and (after the apostasy of Jason li. 2. c. 4. v. 10.) \it Highpriest\it*, lamenting the pitiful state of God's people, with resolute mind, and invincible courage resisting wicked Antiochus (1. Mach. 2.) of just zeal with his own hands \it slew one\it*, who for fear of death \it was ready to offer sacrifice to idols\it*, & withal \it killed the King's commissioner\it*, who came to compel men to commit idolatry: and then gathered troops to defend so holy a cause. Against whom the enemies fighting on the sabbath days killed many, which of scruple would not resist.
\ip But upon further consideration, the rest resolved to defend themselves also on the sabbath day, if they were assaulted.
\ip Next to him succeeded his son \it Judas Machabeus\it* in both the offices of \it High Priest\it* & \it General\it* \f - \ft Judas Machabeus.\f* \it captain\it*: who (as good order required) first \it pursued the wicked\it* (to wit amongst his own subjects) \it inquiring them out, and such as troubled his people, them he burned with fire\it*. 1. Mach. 3. v. 5. \it and his enemies were repelled for fear of him: all the workers of iniquity were troubled: and salvation was directed in his hand\it*. For he & his followers. 2. Mac. 8. v. 2. \it invocated our Lord, that he would\it* \f - \ft A religious prayer.\f* \it have respect to his\it* own \it people; the temple; the city; hear the voice of blood crying unto him, remember the most unjust deaths of innocents, and the blasphemies done to his name. So he with a few\it* (having made this preparation by prayer) \it overthrew the army of Antiochus, with their four principal captains Apollonius\it* (1. Mach. 3. v. 11.) and (v. 23.) \it Seron\it*, (ch. 4.) \it Gorgias\it* and \it Lysias\it*. Then cleansing the \f - \ft The temple cleansed. captivity.\f* temple (v. 36. & l. 2. ch. 10.) renewed the holy vessels, which were destroyed by Antiochus, and dedicated a new altar. v. 47. & 56.
\ip And whiles Judas with his brethren delivered the people from all bordering enemies. 1. Mach. 5. & li. 2. ch. 10. & 11. Antiochus Epiphanes li. 1. c. 6. & li. 2. ch. 9. \it died most miserably\it*. And his \f - \ft Antiochus died miserably.\f* young son \it Antiochus Eupator reigned\it*. Against whose captains Judas had still more victories. li. 2. ch. 12. & 13. Then followed the last battle of \it Nicanor\it* sent by King Demetrius, where he was \f - \ft Nicanor slain.\f* \it slain\it* by Judas' forces, in the midst of his army. li. 1. ch. 7. & li. 2. ch. 15. whereof Demetrius hearing sent new forces with Bacchides and Alcimus, and more than two parts of Judas small camp flying away, he with only eight hundred. li. 1. ch. 9. setting upon the enemies defeated the strongest part of their army: but another part coming at his back great slaughter was made on both sides, and \it Judas\it* after many heroical acts was now \it slain in battle\it*, dying \it with most renowned glory.\it* v. 8. all \f - \ft Judas died gloriously.\f* good men lamenting his death.
\ip After \it him Jonathas\it* his brother succeeded \it Highpriest & general captain\it*, ch. 9. v. 28. who \f - \ft Jonathas.\f* managing the common affairs with great wisdom, piety, and courage, the wicked usurper \it Alcimus\it*, who not long before swearing that he would not hurt the Assideans presently killed threescore of them in one day. li. 1. ch. 7. v. 15. and beginning to deface the temple, was suddenly strucken with a palsy, and \it died miserably\it* li. 1. ch. 9. v. 54. Jonathas prospering against the enemies confirmed \f - \ft Alcimus died miserably.\f* league with the Romans and Lacedemonians, ch. 12. At last was deceived, and both he and his sons were treacherously slain by Tryphon, ch. 13. So \it Simon\it* his brother was made \it Highpriest\it* and \f - \ft Simon.\f* \it captain general\it* by public consent. ch. 14. who after many noble acts, ch. 15. was also villainously slain with two of his sons, by his son in law Ptolomee. And his other son \it Joannes Hyrcanus\it* \f - \ft I oannes Hyrcanus.\f* succeeded, ch. 16. In his days the Jews in Jerusalem writ to their brethren in Aegypt, exhorting them not to frequent the schismatical temple in Aegypt, but to keep the feasts which were instituted in Jerusalem. Thus much of the troublesome state of the Church, reduced to peace by the Machabees.
\ip Shortly after which time, the Roman kingdom having been often increased in the space of near \f - \ft The Roman Monarchy.\f* seven hundred years from the building of Rome, was by Pompeius, subduing the East countries, extended so far, that as Pliny writeth. li. 7. c. 26. Asia Minor was now as it were the middle part, which before was the uttermost borders of their dominions. And the same Pompeius, amongst the rest, taking Jerusalem, brought \it the Jews under the Roman Empire\it*, near fourscore years before Christ. Under whom they enjoyed some liberty, till \it Herod\it* Ascalonita a stranger born (his \f - \ft Herod the first strange King of the Jews.\f* father an Idumean, his mother an Arabic) was first made governor of Galilee, then Tetrarch of Judea, and afterwards King thereof. Who being advanced by the Romans to royal dignity, endeavouring by sundry benefits to get the people's favour, amongst other things enlarged and \f - \ft He enlarged the temple.\f* adorned their Temple, making it as it were a new edifice, in comparison of that which was built after the captivity: yea more excellent as some think, than that which Salomon built. But this \f - \ft But sold the office of High-Priest.\f* new King made sale of spiritual offices. Namely he sold the office of the Highpriest for money. And that from year to year, or for short and limited time. In him was fulfilled the prophecy of \f - \ft The true succession continued also in the times of usurpers.\f* the Patriarch Jacob, Gen. 49. giving it for a sign that Christ our Redeemer should presently come into this world, saying. \it The sceptre shall not be taken away from Judas, and a duke out of his\it* \f - \ft This King was a sign of Christ's coming.\f* \it thigh, till he do come that is to be sent, and the same shall be the expectation of the Gentiles\it*. And therefore Herod hearing by the Sages, that the true King of Jews was born, in extreme fury murdered the innocent Infants. Mat. 2. And so both Jews and Gentiles were admonished that the \it Messias\it* was born \it of the\it* seed and \it right line of King David\it*.
\ip Whose Genealogy before the captivity we noted in the fifth age of the world to \it Joachaz\it* son of Iosias. Now therefore to prosecute the same, we must observe, that whereas S. Matthew saith: \f - \ft The genealogy of Christ from the\f*
\ip \it Iosias begat Jechonias\it*, by this Jechonias he meaneth Joachaz, otherwise called Jechonias; or else he ascribeth the nephew to the grand father as his son. For Iosias was slain at least eleven years before Jechonias the father of Salathiel was born. And this later \it Jechonias\it* was also called \it Joachin, the first of the third Tessaradechad\it*, so the second \it Salathiel\it*, the \it 3. Zorobabel, 4. Abiud, 5. Eliachim, 6. Azor. 7. Sadoc. 8. Achim, 9. Eliud, 10. Eleazar, 11. Mathan, 12. Jacob, 13. Joseph, the husband of Mary, of whom was born\it* the fourteenth, JESUS CHRIST. And this known by tradition, not written before S. Matthew's Gospel.
\ip The succession also of the Highpriests, declared in our former Recapitulations of the fourth and \f - \ft Succession of High-Priests.\f* fifth ages, from Aaron to Josedech, who was High-Priest in the captivity, after that his father Saraias was slain. 4. Reg. 25. v. 18. continued, as partly by holy scriptures, the rest by other authors appeareth in this order: After the same \it Josedech\it*, his son \it Iosue\it*, then \it Ioachim, Eliachim, Eliasib, Joiada, Jonathan, Iaddus\it*, in the time of king Alexander: \it Onias\it* the first, \it Simon Priscus, Eleazarus\it*, by whom the Seventy two Interpreters were sent to King Ptolomeus Philodelphus. \it Manasses\it*, who became an Apostata, \it Onias\it* the third, whose brother Jason obtained the office of the King by simony and became an Apostata, so was never lawful, neither those that followed him. Menelaus of the tribe of Benjamin. Lysimachus his brother & vicar. Alcimus though of Aaron's stock, yet for his Apostasy unlawful. All which time the true High Priests were of the Machabees, \it Matthathias\it* & his sons \it Judas, Jonathas\it*, & \it Simon\it*, his son \it I oannes Hyrcanus\it*. Then \it Aristobolus, Iosue, Simon, Matthias, Iosephus, Iozarus, Eleazarus, Iosue, Anna, Ismael, Eleazarus, Simon\it*, and \it Caiphas\it*. Who in councel (Ioan. 11. v. 49.) gave sentence (which himself understood not) \it that it was expedient, that one man die for the people, and the whole nation perish not\it*. Which the holy Evangelist ascribeth to his office, \it being highpriest of that year, he prophesied that JESUS should die for the nation: and not only for the nation, but to gather into one the children of God, that were dispersed\it*.
\ip JESUS REDEEMER, correct in us our errors, gather the dispersed, conserve them that are and shall be gathered, make all one flock in one fold under one Pastor, thyself JESUS CHRIST. To whom with the Father, and the Holy Ghost be all thanks, praise, honour, and glory, now and for ever and ever. AMEN.
